window.GUIDE_SEARCH = {"checked":"October 2026","entries":[{"slug":"overview.html","number":"","short":"Overview","title":"Free Tools to Run a Small Business / LLC","group":"Overview","headings":[{"id":"who-this-is-for","text":"Who this is for"},{"id":"the-one-rule-that-matters","text":"The one rule that matters"},{"id":"what-s-inside","text":"What's inside"},{"id":"how-to-use-this-guide","text":"How to use this guide"},{"id":"what-free-means-here","text":"What \"free\" means here"},{"id":"safety-rules-for-every-tool","text":"Safety rules for every tool"},{"id":"the-free-stack-at-a-glance","text":"The free stack at a glance"},{"id":"a-note-from-the-maker","text":"A note from the maker"}],"text":"Free Tools to Run a Small Business / LLC A friendly, no-subscription field guide for new LLCs and one-person businesses in Indiana and the US. Built for beginners who need a real operating system, not a 47-tab productivity fantasy. This guide covers the jobs every small business has to do— keep the books, send invoices, remember people, stay in touch, have a website, make things look decent, book meetings, collect answers, sign documents, find trustworthy templates, learn new skills, and get free human help —and shows a genuinely free starting point for each one. Plans and links checked: October 25, 2026. Free plans change. Verify current limits before you build a workflow around them. This is education, not legal, tax, accounting, or financial advice. No attorney-client, accountant-client, or fiduciary relationship is created. Business structures, taxes, contracts, insurance, licenses, and recordkeeping depend on facts we don't have. Check with a licensed professional in your jurisdiction before making decisions. --- Who this is for You are probably one of these people: Starting a one-person LLC or sole proprietorship and trying not to spend $200 a month on software. Already running a tiny business and paying for tools you barely use. An author, creator, freelancer, consultant, reseller, or side-project owner who needs the boring parts to work. In Indiana and wondering what free help actually exists. The examples use US and Indiana resources, but most of the software choices work more broadly. The guide is written in plain English, in the order you're likely to need things. The one rule that matters Choose one home for every job, then make sure you can leave with your data. Tool sprawl is expensive even when the tools are free. Every new login, notification, and dashboard costs attention. The free stack in this guide is deliberately boring: one place for books, one for invoices, one for relationships, one for email, one for your site. Add complexity only when a real limit stops you. What's inside Chapter The job Free starting point --- --------- --------- --------------------- 00 Start here Get the first version of your system working One email, one folder, one calendar 01 Accounting Know what came in and went out Wave Starter 02 Invoicing Ask for money clearly and follow up Zoho Invoice Free or Wave 03 CRM Remember people and follow through HubSpot Free or a careful spreadsheet 04 Email marketing Stay in touch with permission MailerLite Free or Brevo Free 05 Website & hosting Exist on the internet without a subscription GitHub Pages or Netlify Free 06 Design Look coherent without a designer yet Canva Free + system fonts 07 Scheduling Book meetings without the email tennis match Cal.com Free or Calendly Free 08 Forms Collect the right information, not everything Tally Free or Google Forms 09 E-signature Get simple agreements signed and stored DocuSeal Free, with cautions 10 Legal-template resources Find better starting points and ask better questions Indiana, IRS, SBA, and SCORE resources 11 Free training & templates Build skills without paid courses SBA Learning Platform, SCORE, freeCodeCamp 12 Free help in Indiana & the US Get qualified human guidance SBDC, SBA, SCORE, INBiz 13 AI helpers Use AI for drafts, not decisions Free assistant + local AI + privacy rules 14 90-day setup plan Turn reading into an operating system 30–60 minutes a week 15 Sources & fine print Check the official details yourself Official pricing and government pages How to use this guide Read Start here. Do the tiny setup. Don't read nine chapters first. Pick your first bottleneck. Not working invoices? Go to accounting and invoicing. No way for people to find you? Go to website. Don't install everything on day one. Follow the 90-day plan. One weekly work session is enough. Ask for help early. Free advising from the Indiana SBDC or SCORE is not a last resort. It's free infrastructure. See Free help. Recheck the plan pages every quarter. Free tiers mutate. Your system should be exportable and replaceable. What \"free\" means here The software in this guide has a genuinely usable free tier —not a trial, not a demo that watermarks your client work, and not a \"free\" plan that requires a paid add-on for the basic job. That does not mean every part of running a business is free: Payment processing costs money. Invoices are free to send; taking a card usually is not. The processor takes a percentage. Domains cost money. A .com usually costs roughly $10–$25 a year, though prices vary. Free plans have limits. Subscriber caps, monthly document limits, storage caps, and AI-usage caps are normal. Your time is the subscription fee. Setup and maintenance are real costs. Prefer the simpler tool. Free tools can change. A free tier can be reduced. Always know where the export button lives. Certain things deserve paid help. A CPA for a messy tax year, an attorney for a contract that matters, a bookkeeper when the backlog is real. Free tools cannot replace professional judgment. Safety rules for every tool Use a business-only email address and a password manager. Turn on two-factor authentication . Never put Social Security numbers, EINs, bank credentials, health records, or API keys in a public form, document, or AI chat. Don't upload other people's sensitive information to a free tool without a reason and permission. Export your data at least quarterly. Free plans change; your records should not be trapped. Check the privacy policy and data-use terms of any tool that touches customer information. No link from a cold email is automatically official. For Indiana filings, start at INBiz, not at a look-alike service. The free stack at a glance BOOKS Wave Starter $0 INVOICES Zoho Invoice Free / Wave $0 PEOPLE HubSpot Free / spreadsheet $0 EMAIL LIST MailerLite Free / Brevo Free $0 WEBSITE GitHub Pages / Netlify Free $0 DESIGN Canva Free $0 BOOKING Cal.com Free / Calendly Free $0 FORMS Tally Free / Google Forms $0 SIGNATURES DocuSeal Free $0 DOCUMENTS INBiz / IRS / SBA / SCORE $0 TRAINING SBA / SCORE / freeCodeCamp $0 HUMAN HELP Indiana SBDC / SCORE / SBA $0 AI DRAFTS Free chatbot / local AI $0 Paid domain, payment-processing fees, and professional advice may still apply. That's honest. The goal is to keep the monthly software bill at zero , not to pretend business has no costs. A note from the maker This guide is published by ASDALE DYNAMICS, LLC , an Indiana company focused on beginner-friendly AI education, useful books, and practical tools. That means the same philosophy as the rest of the library: plain English, step by step, no condescension, no invented urgency, no secrets or personal data in the templates. Not affiliated with any tool listed. No affiliate links are used. If a free plan changes, the official source linked in each chapter is the source of truth. --- Start with Start here →"},{"slug":"00-start-here.html","number":"00","short":"Start here","title":"Start here: your first working version","group":"Foundations","headings":[{"id":"the-5-things-every-tiny-business-needs-first","text":"The 5 things every tiny business needs first"},{"id":"the-30-minute-start","text":"The 30-minute start"},{"id":"minutes-0-8-make-a-clean-drawer","text":"Minutes 0–8: Make a clean drawer"},{"id":"minutes-8-20-give-the-money-a-home","text":"Minutes 8–20: Give the money a home"},{"id":"minutes-20-30-make-the-first-request-and-first-record","text":"Minutes 20–30: Make the first request and first record"},{"id":"what-to-do-before-forming-or-changing-an-llc","text":"What to do before forming or changing an LLC"},{"id":"the-tool-map","text":"The tool map"},{"id":"set-up-a-backup-rhythm-now","text":"Set up a backup rhythm now"},{"id":"ai-helper-your-set-up-the-boring-parts-prompt","text":"AI helper: your \"set up the boring parts\" prompt"},{"id":"sources-and-official-starting-points","text":"Sources and official starting points"}],"text":"Start here: your first working version Goal: In about 30 minutes, create a small, stable place for business money, business words, and business people. Not a perfect system. A working one. Education, not legal or tax advice. This chapter mentions business structure and records only as organization topics. For decisions about how your business should be formed, taxed, insured, or contracted, use a licensed attorney or CPA, or start with the free advising described in Free help in Indiana & the US. The 5 things every tiny business needs first A business-only email address. Not your personal inbox with a label. Something like hello@yourdomain.com later, or a separate free Gmail/Outlook account now. A business-only money trail. A separate bank account or account you use only for the business. Business banking can have fees; ask about free accounts before applying. A books home. For most US beginners, Wave Starter is the least expensive path because core accounting and invoicing are free. A way to be paid. A simple invoice with clear terms, plus a payment method whose fees you understand. A way to be found or contacted. At minimum, a one-page website and a form or email address. You don't need a brand refresh first. Everything else is an upgrade to those five. The 30-minute start Minutes 0–8: Make a clean drawer Create a business-only email. Create three folders in a cloud drive: Clients & customers , Money , Brand & admin . Inside Money , create 2026 and a monthly folder. Put receipts there. Write one sentence describing what the business does and who it helps. Keep it in a note. This is your reusable introduction. Minutes 8–20: Give the money a home Open Wave and create a free Starter account. Add your business name and basic details. Connect the business bank account if you're comfortable doing so. If you're not ready, start with manual entries and add the connection later. Do not enter years of old transactions. Start from today, or from the beginning of the current tax year after asking a professional what you actually need. Minutes 20–30: Make the first request and first record Create one invoice template. Create one contact for a real or example client. Write down your payment terms: when payment is due, how to pay, and where to ask questions. Put a recurring 30-minute \"Money date\" on your calendar for the same day each month. That is enough for day one. What to do before forming or changing an LLC The guide is about free tools, not entity selection. But since LLC questions come up early, here are organization guardrails—not recommendations about whether or not to form an entity: Indiana filings start with INBiz. It is Indiana's official business filing portal: https://inbiz.in.gov/ . Avoid cold calls and look-alike \"LLC filing\" sites that charge a service fee for something you can file directly. Confirm any fee shown in INBiz before paying. An EIN is free from the IRS. Apply directly at https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/apply-for-an-employer-identification-number-ein-online . Never pay a third party just to \"get an EIN\" unless they're providing a service you understand and want. An LLC is not a free pass on taxes or paperwork. Annual reports, registered agent/office requirements, local licenses, taxes, and insurance can still apply. Ask the SBDC or a professional what applies to your situation. Don't sign a template because it's free. Free legal templates can be useful starting points, but a lease, business contract, operating agreement, or employment document may need an Indiana attorney's review. See Legal-template resources. Don't use a home address without thinking. Public filings may expose an address. Registered-agent options and privacy trade-offs vary. Ask a professional or the Secretary of State. The tool map Job Start with Add later only when… ----- ------------ ---------------------- Bookkeeping Wave Starter You need inventory, payroll, or accountant-specific workflows Invoices Zoho Invoice Free or Wave Your volume exceeds the free invoice limit Relationships HubSpot Free or a spreadsheet You consistently miss follow-ups Email list MailerLite Free or Brevo Free Your list outgrows the free tier Website GitHub Pages or Netlify Free You need a store, membership site, or complex forms Design Canva Free You need paid brand tools or premium assets Booking Cal.com Free or Calendly Free You need team routing and advanced workflows Forms Tally Free or Google Forms You need paid storage or advanced permissions Signatures DocuSeal Free You need higher volume or SMS verification Help SBDC, SCORE, SBA, IRS, INBiz Your question genuinely needs paid expertise Do not add a second tool in a category until the first one actually hurts. Free tools are only cheap if you use them. Set up a backup rhythm now Software is rented. Your records are yours. Monthly: export your accounting data as CSV and a PDF report. Download invoices. Monthly: export contacts or copy the CRM data somewhere you control. Monthly: download a copy of your email list (where the plan allows) and keep it in your private files. Quarterly: download your website source or repository and confirm you can restore it. Quarterly: check current free-plan limits and renewal dates. Whenever you change tools: export before you cancel. Keep the backup in a private folder with access limited to you. Don't post exports to public repositories or AI chats. AI helper: your \"set up the boring parts\" prompt Use a free chatbot, but redact personal and financial details first . Never paste account numbers, EINs, customer data, or documents you don't own. You are a calm operations assistant for a one-person business. Ask me five short questions about how money, customers, and documents currently flow through my business. Then give me a 7-step setup plan that takes no more than 30 minutes per step. Keep the tools free, avoid asking me for sensitive information, and label every guess as [assumption]. End with a one-page monthly maintenance checklist. This is organizational help, not legal, tax, or financial advice. Then verify every recommendation against the tool's official pricing page. Sources and official starting points Indiana INBiz (official business filings): https://inbiz.in.gov/ Indiana Secretary of State, business resources: https://www.in.gov/sos/business/ IRS, Employer Identification Number: https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/apply-for-an-employer-identification-number-ein-online IRS, Small Business and Self-Employed: https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed SBA, free local counseling: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/ Indiana SBDC: https://isbdc.org/ SCORE: https://www.score.org/ Wave pricing: https://www.waveapps.com/pricing Checked October 22, 2026. Plans and official pages can change. Not legal or tax advice. Next: Accounting →"},{"slug":"01-accounting.html","number":"01","short":"Accounting","title":"Accounting: know the numbers without becoming an accountant","group":"Money","headings":[{"id":"the-free-starting-point-wave-starter","text":"The free starting point: Wave Starter"},{"id":"what-good-bookkeeping-actually-looks-like","text":"What \"good bookkeeping\" actually looks like"},{"id":"set-up-wave-in-about-45-minutes","text":"Set up Wave in about 45 minutes"},{"id":"the-25-minute-monthly-money-date","text":"The 25-minute monthly money date"},{"id":"a-note-about-receipts","text":"A note about receipts"},{"id":"free-plan-watch-outs","text":"Free-plan watch-outs"},{"id":"ai-helper-the-monthly-close-buddy","text":"AI helper: the monthly close buddy"},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources"}],"text":"Accounting: know the numbers without becoming an accountant The job: Keep a clean, understandable record of money that came in, money that went out, and what still belongs to whom. You are not trying to produce a tax return by yourself. You are trying to avoid a shoebox in April. Not tax, accounting, or financial advice. Expense treatment, estimated taxes, payroll, sales tax, inventory, home offices, and multi-state issues are fact-specific. Use a CPA or enrolled agent for decisions. Free tools help you organize; they don't replace professional review. The free starting point: Wave Starter Wave offers a free Starter plan in the US and Canada that includes core accounting and unlimited invoicing. Paid add-ons and card processing exist; you do not need them to start bookkeeping. Why it's the recommendation for a very small US business: It's one place for income, expenses, and invoices. It works on a phone and in a browser. The free plan is usable rather than a short trial. It can connect to a bank account, which reduces manual entry. Alternative for a non-US business: Wave's availability varies. Check local free options and local accounting requirements. Do not pretend a US tool handles another country's tax rules. Alternative for a spreadsheet lover: A simple spreadsheet is honest and infinitely exportable. It is not the best choice if you will forget to update it or need bank connections. If you use a spreadsheet, log every transaction, keep receipts, reconcile monthly, and export a copy. What \"good bookkeeping\" actually looks like At minimum, every transaction should answer: Date — when it happened. Description — what it was. Amount — how much. Account/category — what kind of money it was. Receipt or evidence — a document that supports it. Business purpose — a short note for anything unclear. For a one-person business, a monthly rhythm matters more than a fancy chart of accounts. A clean modest set of categories beats 87 micro-categories you can't explain. Set up Wave in about 45 minutes Create the free account. Choose the Starter plan. Dismiss paid add-ons unless you already know you need them. Add the business details. Legal name, address, currency, and logo if you have one. Use your business email. Connect the business account. Read the permissions screen. Wave uses a third-party connection provider; understand what it can see. If you're not ready, skip the connection and add transactions manually this week. Set a simple chart of accounts. Start with broad buckets such as sales, contract income, software, supplies, advertising, transaction fees, professional services, and owner draws/contributions. If you're unsure, leave something labeled \"Ask my accountant\" rather than guessing. Import only the current year. Don't recreate six years of history unless a professional says to. Duplicate transactions create duplicate problems. Categorize 10 transactions. Learn where the review screen lives. Don't aim for perfection; aim for a repeatable monthly pass. Attach your first receipts. Save a PDF or image to your Money/2026 folder. Receipt scanning may be a paid add-on; a phone photo plus a consistent folder is still useful. Create a customer and one invoice. You can learn invoicing in the next chapter. Label owner money correctly. If you take money out of the business, don't call it a mysterious expense. Ask a professional how owner draws/contributions should be recorded for your tax classification. Export a test copy. If you can't find the export screen, you're not ready to trust the tool. Make notes. The 25-minute monthly money date Put it on the calendar. Bring a drink. Do the same steps in the same order. Step 1 — Reconcile (8 min). Match the bank transactions in Wave to reality. Investigate duplicates and missing transactions. The goal is that Wave agrees with the bank. Step 2 — Categorize and annotate (8 min). Clear the review queue. For anything ambiguous, write a question instead of inventing an answer. Save it on a running \"ask my accountant\" list. Step 3 — Check invoices (4 min). Look at unpaid invoices and send a friendly reminder. Record any payment fees. Don't count money as received until it's actually received. Step 4 — Close the month (3 min). Export a CSV of transactions and a simple profit-and-loss report. Download any new invoices and receipts into the monthly folder. Write a one-line summary: \"October: two invoices paid, one late, $X in business software.\" Step 5 — Make one decision (2 min). Choose one small improvement for next month: ask for a deposit, raise a price, cancel an unused tool, or book help. A note about receipts Sophisticated receipt scanning is convenient, but not required on day one. What matters: Save receipts the same week. Name files with date, vendor, and amount. Keep them in a private folder with backups. Match receipts to the transaction list. Ask a professional how long to keep records for your situation. Wave's receipt-scanning feature may require a paid add-on. Check the current pricing page. Also check whether a mobile photo stored in your own folder is enough for your workflow; don't pay for scanning just to create the same result. Free-plan watch-outs Card payments and bank connections are not the same as accounting. Payment processing fees are separate from the free bookkeeping plan. Wave's \"free\" can include upsells. The account is free; payroll, payments, receipts, or other modules may cost money. You can say no. No tax filing included. Wave may offer tax products in some regions, but you remain responsible for filing. A free tool is not a tax preparer. Data export matters. Test the export now, not during an emergency. Don't mix personal and business spending if you can avoid it. A clean account saves hours. Ask your bank about fee-free business options. AI can organize; it cannot sign off. Never ask a chatbot to determine your tax deduction without verifying with a professional. AI helper: the monthly close buddy Use a free AI assistant only after removing names, account numbers, and other sensitive details. Act as a careful bookkeeping assistant, not a CPA. I will paste anonymized descriptions of this month's transactions. Sort them into my existing categories, list anything ambiguous as a question rather than a guess, and create a 20-minute month-end checklist. Do not advise me on tax deductions, filing positions, or whether an expense is deductible. Do not invent amounts or vendors. End with three questions to ask my accountant. Good uses of AI here: explain a confusing report in plain English, draft a checklist, summarize a month you describe, generate a list of questions. Bad uses: paste raw bank statements, ask it to file taxes, ask it to decide if something is deductible, trust its math without checking. Sources Wave pricing: https://www.waveapps.com/pricing Wave: https://www.waveapps.com/ IRS, Small Business and Self-Employed Tax Center: https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed IRS, Self-Employed Individuals Tax Center: https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/self-employed-individuals-tax-center IRS, Recordkeeping for small business: https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/recordkeeping SBA, free counseling: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/ Free plans checked October 22, 2026. Verify current limits and regional availability. Not legal or tax advice. Next: Invoicing →"},{"slug":"02-invoicing.html","number":"02","short":"Invoicing","title":"Invoicing: ask for money clearly, then follow up kindly","group":"Money","headings":[{"id":"the-free-starting-point","text":"The free starting point"},{"id":"best-all-free-standalone-zoho-invoice","text":"Best all-free standalone: Zoho Invoice"},{"id":"best-if-you-already-use-wave-wave-invoices","text":"Best if you already use Wave: Wave invoices"},{"id":"what-a-beginner-friendly-invoice-needs","text":"What a beginner-friendly invoice needs"},{"id":"set-it-up-without-overthinking","text":"Set it up without overthinking"},{"id":"a-simple-follow-up-ladder","text":"A simple follow-up ladder"},{"id":"deposits-late-fees-and-taxes","text":"Deposits, late fees, and taxes"},{"id":"free-plan-watch-outs","text":"Free-plan watch-outs"},{"id":"ai-helper-the-follow-up-draft","text":"AI helper: the follow-up draft"},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources"}],"text":"Invoicing: ask for money clearly, then follow up kindly The job: Send a professional request for payment with unambiguous terms, make paying easy, and follow up without waiting three months and developing a stomachache. Not legal or tax advice. Contract terms, late fees, deposits, sales tax, and what happens when a client doesn't pay depend on your agreements and jurisdiction. Templates and scripts in this guide are educational starting points. For a high-value or disputed invoice, talk to an attorney or CPA. The free starting point Best all-free standalone: Zoho Invoice Zoho Invoice is free for what most one-person businesses need, with limits as of this writing: up to two users, three projects, and 500 invoices per year . It produces professional invoices, estimates, and payment links. It may display \"Powered by Zoho\" branding. Payment gateway fees are separate. Best if you already use Wave: Wave invoices If you set up Wave Starter for bookkeeping, use its invoicing instead of adding a second system. Fewer places for revenue to get lost. Wave's accounting and invoicing are free on Starter, with paid processing and optional add-ons. Pick one home for invoices. Do not invoice from Wave for one client, Zoho for another, and a Canva PDF for a third. That is how records become archaeology. What a beginner-friendly invoice needs Use plain fields and avoid legal-sounding filler you can't explain. Your business name and contact information Your customer's name and contact information A unique invoice number and issue date A clear description of what you delivered (or milestones) Quantity, rate, and total Any tax you're required to charge (ask a professional; don't guess) Payment terms: due date, accepted methods, and who pays processing fees, if consistent with your agreement Deposit or late-fee terms only if they were agreed in advance A link or instructions to pay A short, friendly note: what to do if something looks wrong The word DRAFT until you verify it If you're selling physical goods, include shipping and return information where applicable. If you're selling digital goods or services, make sure the scope and delivery date are clear. Set it up without overthinking Create one branded template. Business name, logo, one accent color, readable font. No decorative clutter. Set numbering. Use a simple consistent scheme, such as 2026-001 , 2026-002 . Set payment terms. Common options include due on receipt, Net 7, Net 14, or Net 30. Pick what you can actually enforce and state it on the invoice. For larger projects, ask about a deposit. Your contract controls; this is not legal advice. Connect a payment method only if needed. Card payments take a percentage. Bank transfer options vary. Clearly distinguish what you receive from what the customer pays. Create a reusable follow-up schedule. Put it on your calendar: due date, +3 days, +7 days, +14 days. Friendly persistence is not rude. Test with a $1 demo invoice to yourself if the tool permits. Confirm the payment page, confirmation email, and receipt work. Download every paid invoice and save it with the matching bookkeeping record. A simple follow-up ladder Tone: warm, specific, and easy to act on. No passive-aggressive poems. Before due (2–3 days early): \"Hi [Name] — just a quick heads-up that invoice 2026-014 for [project] is due Friday. Here's the payment link if you need it. Thanks!\" Due date: \"Hi [Name] — invoice 2026-014 is due today. I've attached a copy. If it's already scheduled, thank you—no need to reply. If something looks off, tell me and I'll take a look.\" 3 days late: \"Hi [Name] — checking in on invoice 2026-014, now three days past due. Could you let me know the expected payment date? I'm happy to resend the link.\" 7–14 days late: \"Hi [Name] — I still don't see payment for invoice 2026-014. Please send a payment date today, or let me know if there's a dispute so we can resolve it. If we agreed on a late fee, note it without inventing new terms.\" After 14 days: pause, check your contract, and decide whether to escalate. If it's a significant amount or there's a dispute, get professional advice rather than sending a threatening template. Good boundaries: stop additional work if your agreement allows it, keep everything in writing, and don't release final files or deliverables you aren't obligated to release. Check the actual contract—not a stranger's internet script. Deposits, late fees, and taxes These are contractual and tax matters. The free-tool guide cannot decide them for you. Practical guardrails: Put terms in the agreement, not only on the invoice. Don't invent late fees after a dispute. If you want late fees, discuss the wording with a professional and include it upfront. Check sales tax. Services and products are treated differently in different places. Indiana businesses should start with the Indiana Department of Revenue and get advice for their specific offerings. Use a CPA or tax pro for 1099 questions. Whether you must issue or file forms is fact-specific. Keep invoices and payments consistent. If the invoice says $500 and the bank shows $487.25 after processing fees, record the fee as a fee—not as an invoice that was only partly paid. Free-plan watch-outs Zoho Invoice is not a full accounting system. Pair it with a bookkeeping workflow, or use Wave for both books and invoices. Don't assume your income is tracked just because you sent an invoice. Zoho's free limits include 500 invoices/year and two users. That's generous for a beginner, but confirm current terms on the official pricing page. Payment links are not free money. The payment processor charges a fee. Read the rate before enabling it. Branding appears on free plans. That is usually fine. Don't pay just to remove it until the business justifies it. The customer's inbox is not your filing cabinet. Download paid invoices and back them up. Free plans can change. Keep the export button on speed dial. AI helper: the follow-up draft Never paste real customer names, bank details, or contract terms you don't own. You are a calm, friendly billing assistant for a one-person business. Draft a payment follow-up email for this situation: [describe timeline, amount, and prior contact in general terms, with no personal data]. Keep it under 110 words. Be warm, specific, and firm. Include one clear next step and a deadline. Do not threaten legal action, invent a late fee, or claim legal rights. Give me two versions: one for a first reminder and one for 14 days overdue. Use it as a draft. Put the real facts in yourself. Verify the due date and contract terms. Sources Zoho Invoice pricing: https://www.zoho.com/invoice/pricing/ Zoho Invoice product page: https://www.zoho.com/invoice/ Wave pricing: https://www.waveapps.com/pricing Indiana Department of Revenue, business tax: https://www.in.gov/dor/i-am-a/business-corp/ IRS, Self-Employed Individuals Tax Center: https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/self-employed-individuals-tax-center SBA, free counseling: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/ Free-plan details checked October 22, 2026; payment rates and tax rules vary and change. Not legal or tax advice. Next: CRM →"},{"slug":"03-crm.html","number":"03","short":"CRM","title":"CRM: remember people without keeping a brain full of sticky notes","group":"Customers","headings":[{"id":"the-free-starting-point","text":"The free starting point"},{"id":"simple-and-free-forever-a-spreadsheet","text":"Simple and free forever: a spreadsheet"},{"id":"when-you-need-a-pipeline-hubspot-free","text":"When you need a pipeline: HubSpot Free"},{"id":"build-a-one-person-pipeline-that-stays-small","text":"Build a one-person pipeline that stays small"},{"id":"the-15-minute-weekly-relationship-review","text":"The 15-minute weekly relationship review"},{"id":"get-consent-right-keep-data-minimal","text":"Get consent right, keep data minimal"},{"id":"set-up-hubspot-free-without-drowning","text":"Set up HubSpot Free without drowning"},{"id":"free-plan-watch-outs","text":"Free-plan watch-outs"},{"id":"ai-helper-turn-notes-into-a-follow-up-plan","text":"AI helper: turn notes into a follow-up plan"},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources"}],"text":"CRM: remember people without keeping a brain full of sticky notes CRM stands for customer relationship management. In a tiny business, it mostly means: know who you talked to, what you promised, and what happens next. You do not need an enterprise platform to do that. Not legal advice. Privacy, marketing consent, record retention, and data protection rules vary. Collect less personal information, protect what you collect, and ask a qualified professional when a customer relationship involves sensitive or regulated data. The free starting point Simple and free forever: a spreadsheet For your first 20–50 relationships, a spreadsheet can be the right CRM. Use columns such as: Name Preferred name and pronouns (optional, for respectful communication) Email / preferred contact method How they found you What they're interested in Last conversation date What I promised What they promised Next action and due date Consent / source of contact Notes (no sensitive data) This is boring, exportable, and easy to understand. The weakness is that it needs manual updating and doesn't remind you by itself. When you need a pipeline: HubSpot Free HubSpot's free CRM stores up to 1,000 contacts for free, with no time limit, and includes core contact and deal management. It is a good next step when you have more conversations than your memory can carry or you want reminders, pipelines, and a shared view of customer activity. Why it can be a good beginner choice: Free contact and deal management. A real interface for follow-ups rather than an infinite spreadsheet. You can start with no paid onboarding. It can grow with you if the business someday needs more. Why it is not automatically the answer: It's a large product with many upsells. A tiny business can get lost in modules it doesn't need. Free-plan limits and features change. It stores customer data in a third-party cloud. You need a reason, permission, and a security habit. HubSpot's broader marketing and sales products can become expensive. The free CRM does not mean the whole platform is free. Start with the simplest tool that makes you follow through. If a spreadsheet works, don't install a CRM to feel professional. Build a one-person pipeline that stays small You need fewer stages than the internet suggests. Try four: New — someone made contact. Talking — a real conversation is happening. Proposal / next step — you've offered something or agreed on a next action. Won or lost — with a one-line reason. Then add a next action and due date to every open contact . A pipeline with no next actions is a museum. The 15-minute weekly relationship review Once a week, open the CRM (or spreadsheet) and do this: Which conversations have no next action? Add one. Who is waiting on me? Send the thing. Who am I waiting on? Send one gentle nudge. Who is a good fit for what I offer now? Send a useful, non-spammy note. Update \"won/lost\" so the pipeline reflects reality. Archive the people who asked not to be contacted. Get consent right, keep data minimal These are practical guardrails, not legal advice: Record where the contact came from. \"Bought a list\" is not a good starting point for permission-based outreach. Don't buy email lists. They damage deliverability and create consent problems. Make unsubscribing or asking not to be contacted easy. Honor requests promptly. Don't collect sensitive data just because you have a notes field. No Social Security numbers, health diagnoses, passwords, or bank credentials. Respect health privacy especially. If you work with healthcare audiences, do not place patient information in an ordinary CRM. HIPAA and related obligations are specialized; get professional guidance. Limit access. Enable two-factor authentication. Don't share a login on a sticky note. Export quarterly. A contact list you can't export is not fully yours. Set up HubSpot Free without drowning Create an account with your business email. Explore Contacts and Deals only. Ignore other navigation for now. Create a four-stage pipeline: New, Talking, Next step, Won/Lost. Add five real contacts that you have permission to store. Skip sensitive notes. Add a next action and due date for each open contact. Connect your business email only if you understand what data the tool will access. Create a recurring 15-minute weekly review. Test export and data deletion before you rely on it. If the idea of setting up a \"pipeline\" makes you sigh, use the spreadsheet. Seriously. A functioning low-tech system beats an abandoned high-tech one. Free-plan watch-outs \"Free CRM\" does not mean free marketing automation. Check the boundaries before uploading your list. Contact limits count contacts, not just customers. Know what happens at the cap and whether duplicates count. Email tracking has privacy implications. Recipients and regulators may care about tracking pixels and consent. Don't enable every tracking feature by reflex. The CRM is not your email marketing platform. Keep sales conversations and consented newsletters in the appropriate tool. No free plan owes you indefinite storage. Back up. Do not treat AI-generated summaries as facts. A confident summary can still be wrong. Check the record. AI helper: turn notes into a follow-up plan You are a careful relationship assistant. Turn these meeting notes into a CRM record using only facts I provide: [paste redacted notes]. Output: two-sentence summary, 2) what I promised, 3) what they promised, one next action with a suggested date, 5) one short, warm follow-up email. Mark anything missing as TBD. Do not invent names, amounts, or commitments. Do not make legal, tax, or medical recommendations. Good uses: organize notes, draft a follow-up, prepare for a call, suggest a polite nudge. Bad uses: store customer secrets in a free chatbot, infer sensitive traits, automate cold outreach, or let it write promises you haven't approved. Sources HubSpot free CRM pricing: https://www.hubspot.com/pricing/crm HubSpot CRM product page: https://www.hubspot.com/products/crm SBA, local counseling: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/ Federal Trade Commission, business guidance: https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance Free limits checked October 22, 2026. Confirm current terms and privacy policies. Not legal advice. Next: Email marketing →"},{"slug":"04-email-marketing.html","number":"04","short":"Email marketing","title":"Email marketing: stay in touch without becoming a spammer","group":"Customers","headings":[{"id":"the-free-starting-point","text":"The free starting point"},{"id":"best-for-a-small-permission-based-list-mailerlite-free","text":"Best for a small permission-based list: MailerLite Free"},{"id":"more-daily-sends-brevo-free","text":"More daily sends: Brevo Free"},{"id":"the-one-thing-that-matters-most-permission","text":"The one thing that matters most: permission"},{"id":"set-up-a-small-email-system","text":"Set up a small email system"},{"id":"a-calm-first-month-rhythm","text":"A calm first-month rhythm"},{"id":"can-spam-guardrails-in-plain-english","text":"CAN-SPAM guardrails in plain English"},{"id":"free-plan-watch-outs","text":"Free-plan watch-outs"},{"id":"ai-helper-build-a-welcome-sequence-that-sounds-human","text":"AI helper: build a welcome sequence that sounds human"},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources"}],"text":"Email marketing: stay in touch without becoming a spammer The job: Send useful, expected emails to people who asked to hear from you; make unsubscribing easy; keep your list portable. An email list is a borrowed audience, not a guaranteed sales funnel. Not legal advice. Marketing-email rules depend on where you and your recipients are. In the US, the FTC's CAN-SPAM guidance is a useful official starting point. If you sell to the EU/UK or handle regulated information, get jurisdiction-specific advice. Never put health records or sensitive personal data in a marketing tool. The free starting point Best for a small permission-based list: MailerLite Free MailerLite currently offers a free plan with up to 250 subscribers and 2,500 monthly emails , plus two user seats. That's a real starting point for a one-person business, but small. You'll outgrow it before you get famous. Good fit: You want a proper signup form, consent record, and unsubscribe handling. You have a growing but small list. You want campaign and automation features without a monthly bill. You can live within the subscriber and monthly-send limits. More daily sends: Brevo Free Brevo currently offers a free plan with 300 emails per day , a drag-and-drop editor, templates, automation, and A/B testing. Free plans and send allowances change; check the official page for current contact and daily limits before moving a list. Good fit: You send small batches frequently. You want to explore a combined email/CRM platform. You can stay under a daily sending ceiling. Don't run both for the same list. Pick one. Duplicates, unsubscribes, and double sends are how trust gets lost. The one thing that matters most: permission A list is not a number. It's a set of people who expect your mail. Build it with: Explicit signups from your website, a form, a workshop, or a direct request. Clear expectations: what you'll send and how often. Double opt-in if your tool supports it. It asks people to confirm by email and reduces typos and bad addresses. A real unsubscribe link. Do not hide it. An honest physical mailing address in commercial email, as required in the US. Use a business mailing address you control; don't use someone else's home without permission. Check the FTC guidance and your tool's help docs. A relationship , not a purchased file. Buying lists is a fast route to spam complaints and deliverability problems. If someone says \"stop emailing me,\" honor it. Don't re-add them because they downloaded a new lead magnet. Keep a do-not-contact record. Set up a small email system Choose one home. Do the free-plan math for the next six months, not just today. Create a signup form. Ask for the minimum: usually an email address and, optionally, a first name. Explain what they'll get. Set the consent language. Keep a dated record of where and when each contact signed up. A checkbox or active opt-in is clearer than a prechecked box. Write a welcome email. It should arrive immediately, confirm what they signed up for, set expectations, and offer one useful next step. Make one helpful monthly email. A practical tip, a link roundup, an update on your book or services, or a question. Not five calls to action. Add a plain-text unsubscribe line. Your provider handles the mechanism; don't bury it. Test it. Send to yourself and a friend. Check mobile, links, images with alt text, footer address, and unsubscribe. Authenticate your domain when you own one. Set up the records your provider recommends (often SPF, DKIM, and DMARC). This is a deliverability practice, not legal advice. Use the provider's current setup guide. Export your list quarterly. You should know what leaving looks like. A calm first-month rhythm Week 1: Welcome email + one useful resource. Week 2: Short note about who you help and one thing you're working on. Week 3: Practical tip or a question you've been getting. Week 4: Review: opens are a weak signal but still a signal. What did people reply to? What caused unsubscribes? Adjust. Metrics to watch without obsessing: delivery, replies, clicks, unsubscribes, and complaints. Open rates are distorted by privacy features and image blocking. A small list with replies is healthier than a giant list that never responds. CAN-SPAM guardrails in plain English The FTC guidance describes requirements including accurate header information, truthful subject lines, clear identification of commercial messages where required, a physical postal address, and a working opt-out mechanism that is honored promptly. There are also rules about third-party sending. Read the official guide; this is not legal advice. Also avoid: Fake \"Re:\" or \"Fwd:\" subject lines. Manufactured deadlines and invented scarcity. Health, financial, or earnings claims you cannot support. Sending to people who didn't ask. Putting customer information or health data in the email content without a proper legal basis. Free-plan watch-outs Subscriber counts include more than customers. Bounced contacts and unsubscribed contacts may count differently. Check the tool. Monthly sends are not the same as unlimited emails. A big launch can hit a limit mid-month. Automation features and support vary by plan. Confirm before designing a workflow. Free-plan branding may be added. Usually acceptable; pay only when the brand cost is real. Cheap does not mean compliant. You remain responsible for consent and accurate claims. Deliverability is not guaranteed because you use a free tool. A warmed domain and relevant content matter. AI helper: build a welcome sequence that sounds human Help me draft a four-email welcome sequence for [describe audience] who signed up for [lead magnet or reason]. Use plain English and short paragraphs. No fake scarcity, no income claims, no health claims. Each email must be under 180 words and contain one clear call to action. Output for each: subject line, preview text, body, and one fact or link I need to verify before sending. End with a checklist for consent, unsubscribe, and a physical mailing address. This is marketing help, not legal advice. Then do the human part: cut the hype, verify every claim, check the footer, and send it to yourself first. Sources MailerLite pricing: https://www.mailerlite.com/pricing MailerLite Free plan: https://www.mailerlite.com/free-plan Brevo pricing: https://www.brevo.com/pricing/ FTC, CAN-SPAM compliance guide: https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/can-spam-act-compliance-guide-business SBA local counseling: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/ Free limits checked October 22, 2026. Verify current pricing, consent rules, and deliverability guidance. Not legal advice. Next: Website & hosting →"},{"slug":"05-website-and-hosting.html","number":"05","short":"Website & hosting","title":"Website & hosting: a small home on the internet","group":"Be found","headings":[{"id":"two-free-hosts-two-different-jobs","text":"Two free hosts, two different jobs"},{"id":"github-pages","text":"GitHub Pages"},{"id":"netlify-free","text":"Netlify Free"},{"id":"honest-comparison","text":"Honest comparison"},{"id":"build-the-smallest-useful-site","text":"Build the smallest useful site"},{"id":"write-like-a-person","text":"Write like a person"},{"id":"publish-without-leaking-secrets","text":"Publish without leaking secrets"},{"id":"forms-analytics-and-privacy","text":"Forms, analytics, and privacy"},{"id":"free-plan-watch-outs","text":"Free-plan watch-outs"},{"id":"ai-helper-edit-the-homepage-without-adding-fiction","text":"AI helper: edit the homepage without adding fiction"},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources"}],"text":"Website & hosting: a small home on the internet The job: Give people one trustworthy place to understand what you do, contact you, and buy only if you actually need to sell online. A small static site is enough for most authors, consultants, freelancers, and service businesses getting started. Not legal advice. Privacy policies, accessibility obligations, advertising claims, and e-commerce requirements depend on your business and audience. Get help when you collect data, sell regulated products, or make claims about outcomes. Never put API keys or other secrets in a public website. Two free hosts, two different jobs GitHub Pages GitHub Pages hosts static sites directly from a GitHub repository, free. It supports custom domains and HTTPS. Use it when: You're comfortable with a repository or willing to learn. Your site is informational: an author hub, documentation, a project page, a portfolio. You don't need server-side form handling, logins, or e-commerce. You're okay with the repository being public on GitHub Free. Publishing a Pages site from a private repository requires a paid GitHub plan; check current docs. Official limits and rules to know: Repositories and published sites have a 1 GB recommended maximum. Bandwidth has a soft 100 GB/month limit and builds a soft 10/hour limit. GitHub says GitHub Pages is not intended or allowed as free hosting to run your online business, e-commerce site, or any other site primarily directed at commercial transactions or providing commercial SaaS . Don't use it for a storefront. Read the current official limits page before choosing it. Don't collect sensitive data through a GitHub Pages site. For a free informational author page or open-source project, GitHub Pages is an excellent fit. For a business site whose main purpose is commercial transactions, choose a host whose terms allow that. Netlify Free Netlify is a static hosting platform with drag-and-drop or Git-based deploys, HTTPS, custom domains, deploy previews, and more backend features. Use it when: You want to drag a folder of files into a browser and get a live site. You want easier custom domain and deploy-preview workflows. You're building a commercial brochure site or need optional form/functions features. You want to experiment without running a server. Current free-plan reality (checked October 22, 2026): The free plan shows a 300-credit monthly limit covering usage such as production deploys, compute, form submissions, bandwidth, and requests. Different actions consume credits. A small static site should fit comfortably, but AI/function features or viral traffic can use more. Netlify's 2024 free-plan announcement described commercial projects as allowed; terms and credit mechanics have evolved, so confirm current pricing and terms before relying on that for a business-critical site. Honest comparison GitHub Pages Netlify Free --- --- --- Setup style Git repository, GitHub Actions or branch Drag-and-drop or Git Custom domain + HTTPS Yes Yes Best for Docs, portfolios, informational pages, open source Small business brochure sites, previews, forms Form handling No built-in backend Some form/function options within plan limits Commercial / storefront Not allowed as primary commercial host Follow current terms; static brochure use is common Main catch Public repo on GitHub Free; commercial-use language Usage credits and changing plan terms A domain is not free. A custom .com usually costs roughly $10–$25 per year, sometimes less for the first year and more on renewal. A .github.io or .netlify.app subdomain is free. Start there if money is tight. Buy a domain when it helps people remember and trust you. Build the smallest useful site Five pages or fewer: Home — who you help, what you offer, one next step. About — a credible short bio, no fake credentials. Work / books / services — what you actually offer, with real details. Contact — email or a form, plus expectations for response time. Privacy & disclosures — what data you collect, if any; affiliate and advertising disclosures where applicable. For an author: add a book page, a simple email signup, and a clear way to request a talk or workshop. For a service business: add scope, starting price or a clear \"request a quote,\" and a booking link. For a reseller: follow the marketplace rules and host only content you own. Write like a person Lead with the customer's problem, not your origin story. Use short headings and real examples. Put contact details where people expect. Tell people what happens after they contact you. Use real testimonials only with permission. Never invent reviews or results. Add alt text to meaningful images; leave decorative images with empty alt. Check color contrast and keyboard navigation. Publish without leaking secrets Start locally. Build or write the files in a private folder. Check for secrets. Search for API KEY , SECRET , passwords, personal addresses, unredacted client files, and analytics tokens you don't recognize. Never commit .env files. Use a public repository only for public content. GitHub history is not a secret vault. Deploy via Netlify Drop or a Git integration. Follow the host's current steps; don't guess an old workflow. Set the custom domain and HTTPS. Follow DNS instructions exactly. Changes can take time to propagate. Test on a phone and with a keyboard. Open every link. Check the contact route. Check the 404 page. Add a privacy note if you collect anything, even an email address through a form. Keep an offline copy. Download the repository or a ZIP of the published files. Your host is not your backup. Forms, analytics, and privacy Static hosts don't automatically give you a database. Use a free form tool such as Tally or Google Forms, or a host's form product, and review where submissions go. Don't collect SSNs, payment details, health information, or passwords through a generic form. If you add analytics, choose a privacy-respecting option, disclose it, and avoid collecting more than you need. Many free analytics tools use cookies; if you're subject to GDPR/UK GDPR, get proper advice on consent. Keep site JavaScript minimal. Never expose an API key in client-side code. If a tool needs a secret, it needs a server or a trusted integration. Free-plan watch-outs Free hosting can have soft limits. A popular post can create a bandwidth bill or site suspension if limits are exceeded. Terms matter more than \"it says free.\" GitHub Pages is explicitly not a free e-commerce platform. A custom domain is a recurring cost. Don't forget the renewal. Email on your domain may be a separate paid service. A domain does not automatically include a mailbox. Free SSL certificates still require configuration. Follow the host's instructions. Don't host a community or customer portal on a static site. Static means no secure login or private data storage. AI helper: edit the homepage without adding fiction Review this homepage copy for clarity, warmth, and accessibility: [paste copy with no customer data]. The audience is [describe]. Rewrite it at a plain-English reading level. Do not invent testimonials, credentials, statistics, or outcomes. Keep a maximum of one primary call to action. Flag any claim that needs evidence. Suggest heading structure and alt-text ideas for images I describe. This is editing help, not legal advice. AI is useful for tightening copy and drafting a privacy page outline—not for making up reviews or promising legal compliance. Check the final content yourself. Sources GitHub Pages: https://pages.github.com/ GitHub Pages limits and prohibited uses: https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/github-pages-limits GitHub Pages visibility requirements for free plans: https://docs.github.com/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/about-github-pages Netlify pricing: https://www.netlify.com/pricing/ Netlify Free announcement (historical context): https://www.netlify.com/blog/introducing-netlify-free-plan/ SBA local counseling: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/ Limits checked October 22, 2026. Hosting terms change; confirm before publishing. Not legal advice. Next: Design →"},{"slug":"06-design.html","number":"06","short":"Design","title":"Design: look put-together without a design degree","group":"Be found","headings":[{"id":"the-free-starting-point-canva-free","text":"The free starting point: Canva Free"},{"id":"understand-the-licensing","text":"Understand the licensing"},{"id":"build-a-tiny-brand-kit","text":"Build a tiny brand kit"},{"id":"starter-palette-recipe","text":"Starter palette recipe"},{"id":"make-the-five-assets-you-actually-need","text":"Make the five assets you actually need"},{"id":"design-rules-that-make-a-beginner-look-experienced","text":"Design rules that make a beginner look experienced"},{"id":"file-organization-that-saves-future-you","text":"File organization that saves future-you"},{"id":"accessible-by-default","text":"Accessible by default"},{"id":"ai-helper-make-a-design-brief-not-a-logo-dump","text":"AI helper: make a design brief, not a logo dump"},{"id":"free-plan-watch-outs","text":"Free-plan watch-outs"},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources"}],"text":"Design: look put-together without a design degree The job: Make your business recognizable and readable across invoices, your website, social posts, and simple documents. Clarity is the design goal. Polish is a bonus. Not legal advice. Image licences, font licences, trademarks, and advertising rules matter. Use assets you have permission to use, don't copy another brand's distinctive identity, and verify licensing before publishing or selling merchandise. The free starting point: Canva Free Canva Free is the easiest free design tool for a beginner. As of October 22, 2026, the free plan includes 5 GB of cloud storage and up to 20 uses of Standard or Premium AI tools , plus millions of free templates and assets. Premium elements are watermarked or require payment; you cannot just export them legally for free. Good first uses: A simple logo lockup (your name in a chosen typeface, not a fake crest). One invoice header and one letterhead. A website hero graphic or open-graph card. Social profile images and a reusable announcement template. A one-page service or book sell sheet. A simple brand sheet for yourself. Understand the licensing Canva's free content is generally available for commercial and noncommercial use under its content licence, but premium content is different , logos and brands can have extra restrictions, and licence terms can change. Read the current Canva licensing explanation and Content License Agreement yourself. Practical rules: Filter for free elements. Don't use a watermarked preview in a live project. Don't use Canva's stock or templates in a way the licence forbids (for example, some standalone asset resale uses). Don't upload someone else's copyrighted character, logo, or photo. Keep source links for any external asset you use. If you sell physical products with a design, check the licence for print-on-demand and merchandise. Build a tiny brand kit You do not need a rebrand. You need consistency. Create a one-page note with: Name and short description. How the name is capitalized. One primary color. A deep green, navy, oxblood, or plain black. Dark text on light backgrounds is easiest. One accent color. Use it for links, buttons, and highlights—not every sentence. Two typefaces at most. One for headings, one for body. System fonts (Georgia, Palatino, Arial, Segoe UI) are free and render everywhere. If using web fonts, check the licence, load locally where possible, and don't depend on an external service for a privacy-first site. One look for images. Photos with the same warmth and light quality, or simple line illustrations. Avoid a collage of unrelated stock. A clear voice. Warm, direct, plain English; no fake urgency. Contact details. Business email, website, and city/state if appropriate. Don't put a private home address on public graphics. Starter palette recipe Use a contrast checker before you publish. For normal text, aim for at least the WCAG AA contrast ratios (4.5:1 for small text; 3:1 for large text). Dark brown-green 17342B on warm cream F8F1E3 is an example of a high-contrast pairing. A bright orange on cream might look nice and still fail—check, don't vibe. Make the five assets you actually need A simple wordmark: your business name set in one font, with good spacing. Skip the clip-art icon for now. A social profile image: name readable at small size. Avoid tiny taglines. A link-preview image: 1200 × 630 px usually works for sharing. Include your name and a short line. An invoice header: logo or name, email, website. Use the same header on quotes. One social/announcement template: duplicate it, change the text, keep the layout. Then stop . A brand is not a 60-page PDF. It's recognition plus trust. Design rules that make a beginner look experienced Hierarchy: one big headline, one supporting line, one call to action. If everything is loud, nothing is. Whitespace: let things breathe. Margins are not wasted space. Alignment: pick a left edge and stick to it. Canva's alignment guides are your friend. Contrast: dark text on light backgrounds, or light text on genuinely dark backgrounds. Don't use yellow text on white. Small text: don't go below sensible body sizes (roughly 16px on the web). On print, no 5-point footnotes. Images: one strong image beats five decorative ones. Provide alt text on the web. Consistency: same colors, same two fonts, same logo placement. Restraint with AI images: don't create fake people who look like your customers or fake product photos. Disclose material use where appropriate. Keep human judgment. File organization that saves future-you Create Brand/ with: Brand/ brand-sheet.md logo/ wordmark-light.png wordmark-dark.png favicon.svg templates/ invoice-header social-square link-preview licenses/ asset-sources.md Export PNG for everyday sharing, SVG or PDF for print-quality logos when available. Keep editable Canva links and a local exported copy. If your Canva account goes away, you should still have the assets you need. Accessible by default Use real text instead of text baked into images when possible. Keep body copy at a comfortable size and line length. Check contrast (a free contrast checker is enough). Add captions/transcripts to video. Don't rely on color alone to communicate (for example, red/green only). Add alt text for informative images: describe the purpose, not every pixel. Make buttons and links look clickable. Test your site with the biggest text setting and keyboard-only navigation. AI helper: make a design brief, not a logo dump Create a one-page design brief for my small business. Ask me five short questions about my audience, the feeling I want, references I like, and where the design will be used. Then propose: one primary color, one accent color, two system-font choices, five layout rules, and a list of five assets I actually need. Do not invent a logo concept or claim the colors pass accessibility—tell me to test contrast with a checker. Keep the tone warm and plain. Use AI for structure, editing, and alternatives. Don't ask it to imitate a living artist's style or reproduce a trademark. Free-plan watch-outs Premium templates and images can trap you at download. Canva Free may let you edit with premium elements but require payment or a watermarked export. Filter to free. AI usage is limited on the free plan. As of this check, up to 20 uses. Don't plan a 30-asset AI campaign on it. Cloud storage is not a backup. Export and back up important assets. Canva is not a trademark search. A name that looks good can still infringe someone else's rights. Free fonts on one site may cost money on another. Check the licence for web, print, merchandise, and embedding. Sources Canva pricing: https://www.canva.com/pricing/ Canva licensing explained: https://www.canva.com/licensing-explained/ Canva Content License Agreement: https://www.canva.com/policies/content-license-agreement/ Canva help, downloaded premium designs: https://www.canva.com/help/download-or-purchase/ WCAG contrast guidance: https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html Free-plan details checked October 22, 2026. Licence terms and AI allowances change. Not legal advice. Next: Scheduling →"},{"slug":"07-scheduling.html","number":"07","short":"Scheduling","title":"Scheduling: end the \"what about Tuesday?\" spiral","group":"Customers","headings":[{"id":"the-free-starting-point","text":"The free starting point"},{"id":"most-flexible-for-one-person-cal-com-free","text":"Most flexible for one person: Cal.com Free"},{"id":"classic-simple-option-calendly-free","text":"Classic simple option: Calendly Free"},{"id":"set-up-a-booking-page-people-can-trust","text":"Set up a booking page people can trust"},{"id":"keep-it-humane-for-you-and-them","text":"Keep it humane for you and them"},{"id":"a-tiny-scheduling-toolkit","text":"A tiny scheduling toolkit"},{"id":"free-plan-watch-outs","text":"Free-plan watch-outs"},{"id":"ai-helper-write-a-booking-invitation-that-respects-everyone-s-time","text":"AI helper: write a booking invitation that respects everyone's time"},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources"}],"text":"Scheduling: end the \"what about Tuesday?\" spiral The job: Let people book a real meeting at a real time without exposing your whole calendar or exchanging nine emails. The best booking page is short, clear, and connected to a calendar you actually keep current. Not legal advice. If you handle appointments for healthcare or other regulated services, don't collect clinical details in a generic booking form. Use systems and policies appropriate to your obligations. This guide covers general business scheduling only. The free starting point Most flexible for one person: Cal.com Free Cal.com is an open-source-friendly scheduling platform with a free plan for one user , unlimited event types , calendar syncing, and integrations. It's a strong choice for a solo business that wants several meeting types—intro call, book-a-session, podcast interview, office hours—without paying per event type. Why it works: One-person plan with unlimited event types. Connects to Google Calendar, Outlook, and other calendars. Good fit for local-first/self-hosting-minded users if you later want more control. White-labeling and larger teams are paid upgrades, not requirements for getting booked. Classic simple option: Calendly Free Calendly's free plan includes one event type , one calendar connection, and a customizable booking page as of this writing. If you offer exactly one kind of meeting—say, a 20-minute discovery call—that's enough. If you need separate 15/30/60-minute meetings, Cal.com's free plan is more flexible. Use Google Calendar or another calendar as the source of truth. The booking tool is the doorbell; the calendar is the house. Set up a booking page people can trust Choose one meeting type. For a first version: \"Intro call — 20 minutes.\" Write a one-sentence purpose. Connect one calendar. Avoid connecting several personal calendars with sensitive event titles unless you understand what's shared. Set availability. Keep it honest. Don't advertise 7 a.m.–9 p.m. if you'll resent every booking. Add buffers. Five or ten minutes between meetings saves the day. Set a minimum notice period (for example, 24 hours) and a booking horizon (for example, the next 30 days). Check time zones. Enable time-zone display. Confirm what the confirmation email shows. Don't book anyone at 3 a.m. by accident. Add location. Auto-generated video link or a clearly stated phone/address. Don't put a private home address unless it's appropriate and safe. Write a short description. What you'll discuss, what to bring, what happens next. Set cancellation and reschedule rules. Include a link for reschedules and a gentle notice request. Let people leave without a guilt trip. Send a confirmation with a clear subject line. \"Confirmed: 20-minute intro call, Tue Oct 27, 2:00 p.m. ET.\" Test it yourself. Book a slot, check the calendar event, click the video link, cancel, and reschedule. Keep it humane for you and them Batch calls when you can. Two or three call slots on certain days is easier than calls scattered through every day. Protect focus time. Mark unavailable blocks in your calendar. Good boundaries prevent bad meetings. Add a pre-meeting form only when it helps. A required form can create friction. Ask only what you need. Don't put intake questions in the event description if they contain sensitive info. Use a secure form and disclose retention. Have a no-show lane. Send one friendly note: \"I missed you today. Want to find another time?\" Then move on. Accessibility: send a link, not just a phone number; use captions when available; offer a contact method for adjustments. Consent: no adding attendees to your newsletter just because they booked a call. A tiny scheduling toolkit Event type: Intro call (20 min) Purpose: Understand the project and decide the next step Location: Video link generated by the tool Availability: Tue/Thu, 10:00–14:00 local, with 10-minute buffers Notice: 24 hours minimum Reschedule: allowed up to 12 hours before Who brings what: a short description and the one decision you want to make Write this in a note and reuse it. Don't reinvent your meeting policy every week. Free-plan watch-outs Cal.com's free plan is for one user. Team routing and advanced branding are paid. Calendly's free plan is one event type. Multiple meeting types or advanced workflows can require a paid plan. The scheduling tool is not your calendar. If you don't maintain the calendar, you'll double-book. Video conferencing may have its own limits. Google Meet, Zoom, and Microsoft Teams free options change. Check current host and participant limits. Booking data lives in a third party. Read the privacy terms and don't collect health details unnecessarily. \"Unlimited event types\" doesn't mean unlimited everything. Integrations, workflows, or branding may still have limits. AI helper: write a booking invitation that respects everyone's time Draft a short booking email for [a specific type of meeting]. My booking page is [describe, not paste a private link]. Offer two relevant time windows in [time zone], include a 45-minute reschedule option, and state that the meeting is [length]. Ask for one piece of context in advance. Keep it under 100 words and warm, not salesy. Do not claim anyone else's availability unless I give it to you. Use AI for wording, not for inventing available times. The calendar knows the truth. Sources Cal.com pricing: https://cal.com/pricing Cal.com product: https://cal.com/ Calendly pricing: https://calendly.com/pricing Google Calendar help: https://support.google.com/calendar/ SBA local counseling: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/ Free-plan details checked October 22, 2026. Confirm current limits and integrations. Not legal advice. Next: Forms →"},{"slug":"08-forms.html","number":"08","short":"Forms","title":"Forms: ask less, get better answers","group":"Customers","headings":[{"id":"the-free-starting-point","text":"The free starting point"},{"id":"tally-free","text":"Tally Free"},{"id":"google-forms-free","text":"Google Forms Free"},{"id":"what-belongs-in-a-beginner-form","text":"What belongs in a beginner form"},{"id":"build-a-form-that-gets-completed","text":"Build a form that gets completed"},{"id":"data-hygiene-for-one-person-businesses","text":"Data hygiene for one-person businesses"},{"id":"free-plan-watch-outs","text":"Free-plan watch-outs"},{"id":"ai-helper-turn-a-messy-list-of-questions-into-a-decent-short-form","text":"AI helper: turn a messy list of questions into a decent short form"},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources"}],"text":"Forms: ask less, get better answers The job: Collect the information you need for a quote request, intake, feedback survey, or application—without becoming a data hoarder. A good form is short, accessible, and clear about where the answers go. Not legal or tax advice. Privacy, consent, data-retention, healthcare, children's data, and payment-card rules are specialized. When in doubt, collect less and get professional guidance. Never ask for Social Security numbers, passwords, full card numbers, or medical details through a generic form. The free starting point Tally Free Tally offers unlimited forms and submissions on its free plan, with features such as logic, integrations, and payments, and Tally branding. It's a clean, beginner-friendly form builder and often the best fit for a small business that needs a professional-looking intake form for free. Free file uploads: individual files up to 10 MB on the free plan as of this writing. Check current storage and submission terms. Perks: conditional logic, answers in a spreadsheet-like view, integrations, and a simple editor. Catch: free-plan branding, and advanced features or higher limits may require a paid plan. Google Forms Free Google Forms is free with a Google account. It's basic, familiar, and stores responses in Google Sheets or Drive. It's a good choice if you already live in Google Workspace-adjacent tools and need a simple questionnaire. Strengths: quick setup, spreadsheet responses, simple sharing. Weaknesses: less control over advanced logic and design; data lives in a third-party ecosystem; account security matters. Watch: consumer Gmail accounts and business accounts have different data/administration implications. Don't put sensitive records in a personal form. Storage limits: Google's help documentation lists a 1 GB total storage limit and a 500,000 responses per form limit for Forms starting September 2026. File uploads can consume that storage quickly. Check the current limit before using Forms as a file-intake system. Pick one. Don't collect the same answers in two forms and then manage two incompatible spreadsheets. What belongs in a beginner form Use exact, neutral labels. Make the required minimum obvious. Examples: For a quote request Name and preferred contact method Email What do you need help with? (long answer) Desired deadline and budget range (optional, if appropriate) How did you find me? Consent checkbox: \"OK to contact me about this request.\" For a workshop interest list Name (optional) Email One question you want answered Consent checkbox with a link to how you'll use the email For feedback One rating, one open question, and one optional contact field Thank-you page with next steps Do not ask for a birthdate, home address, income, diagnosis, or ID number unless there is a specific, legitimate business reason and a safe system. A form is not a secure vault. Build a form that gets completed Write the decision first. \"What will I do with this response?\" If there's no action, remove the question. Name the form in plain language. \"Project request\" beats \"Inquiry Intake v3.\" Use one page for a short form. Long forms need sections; short forms need respect. Use clear field labels. Labels above fields, not placeholder-only text that disappears. Mark optional fields as optional. Don't make everything required. Add help text for confusing questions. \"Budget range helps me scope this\" is friendlier than an unlabelled dropdown. Explain privacy. Where will responses go? Who sees them? How long will you keep them? Add a confirmation message. Tell people what happens next and when to expect a reply. Test accessibility. Keyboard navigation, visible focus, error messages, and sufficient contrast. Test the notification and export. Submit a fake entry, receive the email, find the row, export a CSV. Data hygiene for one-person businesses Use a business account with two-factor authentication. Limit access. Don't share the admin login. Review responses on a schedule. Turn off collection when the campaign ends. Export and archive what you need for your records, then consider deleting unnecessary form submissions. Don't use a form for e-signatures. A typed \"I agree\" field in a general form isn't a complete signing workflow. Use a dedicated e-signature tool and get legal advice for important agreements. Avoid healthcare data. If you educate healthcare workers, keep educational feedback separate from patient information. Never collect PHI through a free form. Be careful with file uploads. Accept only the formats you need, set size expectations, and never allow executable files. Watch third-party integrations. Every connected app is another place data can leak. Spam happens. Add a simple challenge or accept that you'll delete junk. Don't collect extra personal data just to \"verify everyone.\" Free-plan watch-outs \"Unlimited submissions\" does not mean unlimited uploads or features. File sizes, storage, and advanced capabilities vary. Free branding appears. That can be a trust signal for a small business, not an emergency. Personal Google accounts lack business-grade admin controls. Fine for low-risk; not for sensitive data. Deleting a form may not immediately delete every response copy. Check the help docs and maintain your own retention plan. Third-party form tools process data on your behalf. Read the privacy policy if you collect customer data. AI helper: turn a messy list of questions into a decent short form I need a form for [purpose]. Here are my rough questions: [list]. Rewrite them as clear, accessible labels, remove anything that is not necessary for this purpose, and mark optional fields. Flag any request for sensitive personal data and suggest a safer alternative. Produce a short consent sentence, a confirmation message, and a one-page internal data-retention note. Do not claim that this makes me compliant with any privacy law. Then delete at least one question. That's not an AI instruction; it's a form-design rule. Sources Tally pricing: https://tally.so/pricing Tally file uploads help: https://www.tally.so/help/file-uploads Google Forms help: https://support.google.com/docs/answer/6281888 Google Forms, response and storage limits: https://support.google.com/docs/answer/139706?hl=en Google Forms product page: https://workspace.google.com/products/forms/ FTC, protecting personal information: https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/protecting-personal-information-guide-business SBA local counseling: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/ Free-plan details checked October 22–25, 2026. Verify current storage and privacy terms. Not legal advice. Next: E-signature →"},{"slug":"09-e-signature.html","number":"09","short":"E-signature","title":"E-signature: get simple documents signed without paper","group":"Paperwork","headings":[{"id":"the-free-starting-point-docuseal","text":"The free starting point: DocuSeal"},{"id":"what-an-e-signature-is-and-isn-t","text":"What an e-signature is—and isn't"},{"id":"set-up-a-small-signing-workflow","text":"Set up a small signing workflow"},{"id":"signature-caution-checklist","text":"Signature caution checklist"},{"id":"free-plan-watch-outs","text":"Free-plan watch-outs"},{"id":"ai-helper-understand-before-you-sign-or-ask-better-questions","text":"AI helper: understand before you sign—or ask better questions"},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources"}],"text":"E-signature: get simple documents signed without paper The job: Send a document for signature, keep a record of who signed and when, and save the completed copy where you can find it. Electronic signatures are ordinary business practice, but the tool is not a lawyer. This chapter is not legal advice. Whether an electronic signature is enforceable, what notices are required, and which documents can be signed electronically depend on the document, the parties, and the law. Federal ESIGN and Indiana's Uniform Electronic Transactions Act generally recognize electronic signatures, but there are important exceptions and practical proof requirements. Ask a licensed attorney for anything consequential. Never use a generic tool for wills, court filings, or documents where a professional has told you not to. The free starting point: DocuSeal DocuSeal is an open-source e-signature platform. Its hosted free plan is small—currently 10 emailed documents per month , with unlimited templates—and it also offers a self-hosted option via the open-source project. For a one-person business that signs a few simple client agreements a month, the free plan can be genuinely useful. Why it's a good fit: Simple templates and signing fields. An audit trail and completed PDF. Open-source code available for self-hosting if you have the technical ability. No need to pay for basic signing on day one. Why self-hosting is not \"free\" for everyone: You need to run and secure a server. You own patches, backups, uptime, and access control. Misconfigured self-hosted software can expose private contracts. Email deliverability and SMS verification may require extra services. Self-hosting is a technical project, not a beginner requirement. If you don't need an online workflow yet, a PDF emailed with clear instructions and a returned signed copy can work for some low-risk documents. That doesn't remove the need for an appropriate agreement or proof; ask a professional when signatures must meet a specific standard. What an e-signature is—and isn't An electronic signature is a way to show intent to sign a record electronically. It is not just a typed name in a contact form. A useful workflow records: The document that was presented The signer's identity/email and intent The time and date Consent to do business electronically, where appropriate The final signed document plus an audit certificate A copy provided to each signer A retention plan for your business records The tool helps with the mechanics. The agreement and the circumstances determine enforceability. This guide can't tell you whether a particular document qualifies. For significant agreements, use an attorney. Set up a small signing workflow Decide if this document should be signed online. Wills, certain family/court documents, and some notices may be excluded or need specific formalities. Ask a lawyer if you're unsure. Use a document you have the right to use. Don't upload someone else's contract without permission. Create a template only for repeatable, low-risk documents. Examples: a simple client agreement your attorney has reviewed, a speaking release, or a basic author-services schedule. Add fields deliberately. Signature, date, initials, name, and maybe an agreed checkbox. Don't ask for extra sensitive information. Set signing order. Who signs first? Does the other party sign after you? Write a plain-language email. Identify the document, why it's being sent, and who to ask with questions. Don't pressure anyone to sign immediately. Test with your own email. Sign a dummy document. Check the final PDF, audit trail, and confirmation messages. Save the completed document. Put it in a private folder with a date and descriptive name. Back it up. DocuSeal is not your permanent records system. Give each signer a copy. Keep the final PDF and any certificate. Don't rely solely on a dashboard link. Delete or archive drafts according to your retention plan. If a document contains personal information, limit who can access it. Signature caution checklist Is this document consequential enough that a professional should draft or review it? If yes, stop. Are all pages present, legible, and the correct version? Do the names, dates, addresses, and payment terms match reality? Is each signer authorized to sign for their side? Is the signature placement correct? Does the signer receive a completed copy? Does the audit trail identify the signer and timestamp? Have you stored it privately and backed it up? Have you avoided collecting ID numbers or health data that you don't need? Would you be comfortable explaining the process in a dispute? If not, get advice. Free-plan watch-outs 10 documents/month is a real ceiling. Count envelopes, not pages. Check what DocuSeal counts before sending a batch. Branding and advanced features may be limited. That's okay for a small operation. Self-hosting transfers responsibility to you. Include server, backup, security, and maintenance costs in the decision. Free tools do not provide legal review. A signature does not make a bad agreement good. Completed signatures contain personal data. Don't put them in public GitHub repos, unencrypted shared drives, or AI chats. SMS verification may cost extra or be unavailable. Confirm the workflow you need. AI helper: understand before you sign—or ask better questions Explain this contract excerpt in plain English, sentence by sentence: [paste an excerpt you own, redacting names and sensitive details]. List the deadlines, payment terms, cancellation/termination terms, deliverables, and anything ambiguous. Then give me 8 questions to ask the other party or an Indiana business attorney. Do not tell me whether the contract is enforceable, whether to sign it, or what the law requires. Do not draft a new contract. This is for my understanding only. AI can help you notice words. It cannot take responsibility for a legal decision. Sources DocuSeal: https://www.docuseal.com/ DocuSeal pricing: https://www.docuseal.com/pricing DocuSeal open-source repository: https://github.com/docusealco/docuseal FTC testimony on ESIGN (background): https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/prepared-statement-federal-trade-commission-esign Indiana Uniform Electronic Transactions Act, IC 26-2-8 (official Indiana Code via the Indiana General Assembly; navigate to Title 26, Article 2, Chapter 8): https://iga.in.gov/laws/current/ic/titles/26/ SBA local counseling: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/ Free-plan details checked October 22, 2026. E-signature law is fact-specific. Not legal advice. Next: Legal-template resources →"},{"slug":"10-legal-templates.html","number":"10","short":"Legal templates","title":"Legal-template resources: start with official forms, not random PDFs","group":"Paperwork","headings":[{"id":"a-hierarchy-of-trust","text":"A hierarchy of trust"},{"id":"the-core-official-resources","text":"The core official resources"},{"id":"1-indiana-formation-reports-and-entity-records","text":"1. Indiana formation, reports, and entity records"},{"id":"2-federal-tax-and-identification-forms","text":"2. Federal tax and identification forms"},{"id":"3-indiana-tax-and-employer-registrations","text":"3. Indiana tax and employer registrations"},{"id":"4-free-business-templates-and-education","text":"4. Free business templates and education"},{"id":"template-map-for-a-tiny-business","text":"Template map for a tiny business"},{"id":"an-operating-agreement-specifically","text":"An operating agreement, specifically"},{"id":"how-to-vet-any-free-template","text":"How to vet any free template"},{"id":"what-to-bring-to-an-attorney","text":"What to bring to an attorney"},{"id":"ai-helper-prepare-for-a-legal-conversation","text":"AI helper: prepare for a legal conversation"},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources"}],"text":"Legal-template resources: start with official forms, not random PDFs The job: Find trustworthy starting points for routine filings and documents, know what a template cannot do, and prepare better questions for a licensed professional. This chapter is education, not legal advice and not a substitute for an attorney. Filing a form or using a template does not create a lawyer-client relationship. Contract, employment, privacy, tax, and entity questions depend on specific facts. The sources below are official or established starting points, but you must verify that a form is current and appropriate for your situation. A hierarchy of trust Use documents in this order, and only when you understand what you're filing: Official government forms and portals for filings the government actually requires. Official guidance that explains the filing process, deadlines, and common mistakes. Reputable educational templates from SBA resource partners, clearly labeled as generic. Templates from commercial providers —read the licence, terms, state-specific disclaimers, and marketing claims carefully. A document a licensed attorney drafted or reviewed for your situation , when the stakes justify it. A random PDF with a professional-looking header is at the bottom of that hierarchy. Free does not mean accurate or state-specific. The core official resources Indiana formation, reports, and entity records INBiz is Indiana's official business filing portal: https://inbiz.in.gov/ . Use it to form an entity, file entity reports, update information, and search business records. The Indiana Secretary of State's business resources are at https://www.in.gov/sos/business/ . Indiana requires entities to maintain a registered agent and registered office in the state. Confirm current requirements in official guidance and ask a professional if you're unsure how privacy, virtual agents, or commercial registered agents interact with your situation. Don't pay an unfamiliar third-party \"LLC filing service\" from a cold call before checking INBiz and understanding what the service actually adds. Important: Filing formation documents is not the same as having a complete operating agreement, licenses, permits, tax registrations, insurance, or good contracts. You may not need all of those immediately, but \"I formed an LLC\" is not a full legal foundation. Federal tax and identification forms EIN online: free and official at https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/apply-for-an-employer-identification-number-ein-online . Form W-9: official PDF and instructions at https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-w-9 . You provide a W-9 to a requester who needs your taxpayer identification number; this page says not to send the form to the IRS. Small business and self-employed center: https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed . Schedule C: https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-schedule-c-form-1040 . Self-employed tax center: https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/self-employed-individuals-tax-center . Publication 334, Tax Guide for Small Business: https://www.irs.gov/publications/p334 . Ask a CPA or enrolled agent which forms and elections apply to you. An LLC is not a tax classification by itself. Do not let an AI or a template decide your tax status. Indiana tax and employer registrations Indiana Department of Revenue, business taxes: https://www.in.gov/dor/i-am-a/business-corp/ . INTIME (Indiana Taxpayer Information Management Engine) is linked from DOR guidance for business tax filing. If you hire anyone, there may be state registration, withholding, unemployment insurance, and workers' compensation questions. Do not copy an employment package from the internet. Use official guidance and a professional. Free business templates and education SCORE resource library: https://www.score.org/resources/ . SCORE offers free business mentoring and a library of articles, templates, and tools. Templates are generic—check dates, assumptions, state-specific issues, and whether the licence allows your use. SBA Business Guide: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/ business-guide . Start here for business planning, launch, management, and funding basics. SBA local assistance: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/ . SBA resource partners can provide free or low-cost counseling, but they do not replace your lawyer or CPA. Indiana SBDC: https://isbdc.org/ . No-cost advising and training, including business planning and referrals. Template map for a tiny business Need A reasonable starting point What a template can't do --- --- --- Form an Indiana LLC INBiz official filing Tell you whether an LLC is right for you or handle taxes/licenses EIN IRS EIN application Choose your tax classification or answer state registration questions Record customer TIN IRS W-9 form Decide whether you need one or what to do with it Business plan / forecast SBA and SCORE templates Guarantee the assumptions are realistic Client agreement / SOW A starting point reviewed by an attorney Fit your scope, liability, payment, IP, and Indiana law NDA Attorney-reviewed template Tell you whether the protection is meaningful Lease / office or studio Commercial lease review by an attorney Protect you from terms you didn't notice Website privacy policy/terms A draft that reflects your actual data practices, reviewed by counsel Make you compliant by itself Employment documents Official agency guidance + an employment attorney Cover worker classification, wage/hour, safety, and benefits Wills, court filings, healthcare directives Appropriate legal process and professional advice Be handled casually with a monthly e-sign plan No legal decision by checklist. Use this map to know what to search for and what to ask, not as a green light. An operating agreement, specifically An operating agreement is generally an internal document that describes how an LLC is run and how owners relate to it. The rules and practices around them vary, and the document isn't a generic form you can assume is right. Ask an Indiana business attorney: Do I need an operating agreement for my situation? What should it say about ownership, capital, distributions, decision-making, deadlocks, transfer, and dissolution? Does it need to be filed or kept internal? How should it interact with my tax elections and estate plan? What happens if I'm a single-member LLC? Free templates can be a place to collect questions, not a final answer. This guide doesn't tell you whether you need one. How to vet any free template Who published it? Government? Bar association? Established nonprofit? Named law firm? Anonymous SEO site? When was it updated? A 2014 template can be worse than no template. Is it state-specific? Generic wording may conflict with Indiana rules. What licence applies? Can you use it commercially? Can you modify it? Are there attribution requirements? What does it leave out? Templates rarely know your scope, risks, compliance duties, or negotiation. Is the layout hiding traps? Check all pages, footnotes, and cross-references. Does it contain placeholders you don't understand? Don't sign or send it until you do. Does it ask for sensitive data you shouldn't collect? Limit data and secure storage. Would you be comfortable reading it aloud to the other person? If not, get help. What to bring to an attorney A one-page description of the business and the decision you're making. The draft document, clearly marked up with your questions. Names of the parties, relationship context, and timeline. Your budget and desired outcome. Relevant emails or prior agreements, redacted as appropriate for secure transmission. A list of questions ranked from \"must understand\" to \"nice to know.\" Not every legal problem needs a full engagement. Some attorneys offer short consultations, flat-fee reviews, or unbundled document review. Ask about cost and scope upfront. Your local bar association can help you find an appropriate lawyer. Free business advising from SCORE or the SBDC can help you prepare, but they are not your lawyer. AI helper: prepare for a legal conversation I am preparing questions for a licensed Indiana business attorney about [describe the document or decision, without sensitive personal details]. My business is [one sentence]. I do not want you to draft a legal document or tell me what the law is. Produce: 1) a one-page context summary I can review, 2) eight specific questions to ask, 3) the documents I should bring, 4) five terms I should ask them to explain in plain English, and the facts I should verify before the meeting. Remind me to redact sensitive information before sharing anything. AI is a preparation aid. The attorney provides advice based on your facts and the actual law. Sources Indiana INBiz: https://inbiz.in.gov/ Indiana Secretary of State, business resources: https://www.in.gov/sos/business/ INBiz, start a business: https://inbiz.in.gov/start-business IRS, EIN: https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/apply-for-an-employer-identification-number-ein-online IRS, Form W-9: https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-w-9 IRS, small business and self-employed: https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed Indiana Department of Revenue, business: https://www.in.gov/dor/i-am-a/business-corp/ SBA Business Guide: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/ business-guide SCORE resources: https://www.score.org/resources/ Indiana SBDC: https://isbdc.org/ Checked October 22, 2026. Government forms and links can change. Verify with the issuing agency. Not legal or tax advice. Next: Free training & templates →"},{"slug":"11-free-training.html","number":"11","short":"Free training","title":"Free training & templates: learn the skill, not just collect the certificate","group":"Learn & get help","headings":[{"id":"the-learning-rule-that-saves-the-most-time","text":"The learning rule that saves the most time"},{"id":"free-training-worth-knowing","text":"Free training worth knowing"},{"id":"government-and-nonprofit-training","text":"Government and nonprofit training"},{"id":"free-vendor-academies-worth-the-time","text":"Free vendor academies worth the time"},{"id":"free-technical-and-ai-learning","text":"Free technical and AI learning"},{"id":"free-templates-that-are-safe-to-start-with","text":"Free templates that are safe to start with"},{"id":"a-four-week-learning-plan-you-can-actually-finish","text":"A four-week learning plan you can actually finish"},{"id":"how-to-vet-free-training","text":"How to vet free training"},{"id":"ai-helper-turn-a-course-into-practice","text":"AI helper: turn a course into practice"},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources"}],"text":"Free training & templates: learn the skill, not just collect the certificate The job: Build the specific skills your business actually needs—bookkeeping basics, marketing, a little web work, AI literacy—without buying a $499 course you never finish. Templates are part of the same job: they give you a decent starting point so you can spend your energy on the parts that are uniquely yours. This is education, not legal, tax, or financial advice. A course, certificate, or template does not make you a lawyer, accountant, or licensed professional. Training quality and free offers change. Check the provider's official page before you invest time. Never pay an unsolicited \"training\" provider that guarantees income or government approvals. The learning rule that saves the most time Learn one skill that removes a current bottleneck, practice it on your real business, then stop. Collecting courses feels productive. Applying one lesson to a real invoice, a real webpage, or a real customer email is what changes the business. A 45-minute session with a blank page beats a 40-hour video library you bookmark and avoid. Free training worth knowing Government and nonprofit training SBA Learning Platform — free, self-paced courses on starting, planning, marketing, finance, and managing a business: https://learning.sba.gov/ . SCORE — free mentoring plus free webinars, courses, and a large resource library: https://www.score.org/ and https://www.score.org/resources/ . Indiana SBDC — no-cost one-on-one advising and training for Indiana businesses: https://isbdc.org/ . IRS Small Business and Self-Employed Tax Center — official guides, forms, and recordkeeping basics: https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed . FTC Business Guidance — plain-language consumer-protection and advertising rules: https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance . DigitalLearn — free, beginner-friendly computer and internet basics from the Public Library Association: https://www.digitallearn.org/ . Your local public library — many libraries offer free access to online course platforms, research databases, and in-person tech help. Ask your branch what your card includes; offerings vary. Free vendor academies worth the time These are run by companies that sell software. That does not make them useless—it means you should expect product-focused lessons and upsells. Provider Good for Official starting point --- --- --- Google Skillshop Analytics, ads, productivity https://skillshop.withgoogle.com/ Microsoft Learn Office, Windows, cloud basics https://learn.microsoft.com/training/ HubSpot Academy Marketing, CRM, content https://academy.hubspot.com/ Canva Design School Simple design and branding https://www.canva.com/designschool/ Meta Blueprint Social media marketing https://www.facebook.com/business/learn Amazon Seller University Reselling on Amazon https://sell.amazon.com/learn eBay Seller Center Reselling on eBay https://www.ebay.com/sellercenter Etsy Seller Handbook Selling handmade or digital goods https://www.etsy.com/seller-handbook Kindle Direct Publishing Help Self-publishing basics https://kdp.amazon.com/en US/help Free technical and AI learning freeCodeCamp — free coding curriculum with projects: https://www.freecodecamp.org/learn/ The Odin Project — free, project-based web development: https://www.theodinproject.com/ Kaggle Learn — short, practical data and AI courses: https://www.kaggle.com/learn Harvard CS50x — free introductory computer science course: https://cs50.harvard.edu/x/ MIT OpenCourseWare — free course materials across many subjects: https://ocw.mit.edu/ Elements of AI — free, non-technical introduction to AI concepts: https://www.elementsofai.com/ Hugging Face Learn — free courses on machine learning and language models: https://huggingface.co/learn Anthropic courses — free educational notebooks on working with AI models: https://github.com/anthropics/courses Coursera, LinkedIn Learning, and similar platforms may let you audit or preview some courses for free, but access rules and certificates vary. Read the current terms; don't assume \"free\" means the graded certificate is free. Free templates that are safe to start with Need Free starting point What to check --- --- --- Business plan outline SBA Business Guide and SCORE templates Update assumptions; don't copy someone else's market data Cash-flow forecast SCORE or SBA template, or a simple spreadsheet Make the formulas visible and test them Client agreement or NDA A template reviewed by an attorney (see Legal templates) Scope, payment, liability, intellectual property, Indiana law Invoice Zoho Invoice Free or Wave Tax lines, payment terms, numbering Social graphics Canva Free templates Filter to free elements; check licences Link-preview image Canva Free, 1200 × 630 px Readable text at small size Documents and letters Microsoft Create templates Remove placeholder text and tracked changes Spreadsheet CRM A sheet you build with the columns in CRM Exportability and privacy Email signup form MailerLite or Brevo template Consent language, footer address, unsubscribe Book-publishing checklist KDP Help and your own outline Royalties, file formats, tax interview, metadata Templates are not strategy. A business plan template with optimistic numbers is still optimistic. A contract template that doesn't match your scope is still a contract. Use templates to organize thinking, not to replace it. A four-week learning plan you can actually finish One 45-minute session per week. Pick a real task, not a certificate. Week 1 — Money literacy (45 min). Watch one SBA or SCORE session on cash flow. Then open your own numbers and write down three questions. Book a free SBDC or SCORE appointment to ask them. Week 2 — Marketing that fits you (45 min). Take one HubSpot Academy or Google Skillshop lesson. Write one useful email or one service page using what you learned. Publish it. Week 3 — Your website or design (45 min). Use one Canva Design School lesson to fix one real asset: homepage header, invoice header, or social profile. Export it and use it. Week 4 — AI with boundaries (45 min). Read AI helpers and set up one assistant for low-risk drafting. Write your redaction rules. Test one prompt on a real, non-sensitive task. At the end, write one sentence: \"I learned and used it to .\" If you can't fill in the second blank, the course was entertainment, not training. How to vet free training Who made it? Official agency, established nonprofit, recognized company, or anonymous funnel? When was it updated? Tax, platform, and marketing advice goes stale quickly. Is there a real exercise? Watching is not the same as doing. What is the upsell? A free lesson that ends in a $2,000 coaching pitch is an advertisement, not a course. Does it promise income or approvals? \"Guaranteed\" is a warning sign, not a feature. Does the certificate mean anything? Usually it documents attendance, not professional licensure. Don't put a vendor certificate in a bio as if it were a degree or credential. Is your data safe? Don't upload customer records, health information, or financial account numbers to an unknown learning platform. Can you use the template commercially? Read the licence. \"Free\" often means personal use only. AI helper: turn a course into practice I have 45 minutes to learn [skill] for my small business. My current bottleneck is [describe the problem in general terms]. Build a practice- first session: 10 minutes of core concepts, 25 minutes of a real exercise using a made-up example, and 10 minutes of reflection. Give me the exercise steps, a checklist to judge my own work, and three questions to ask a free SBDC or SCORE advisor. Do not invent statistics or links. Do not promise income results. This is learning help, not legal, tax, or financial advice. Use AI to structure practice. Then verify any factual claim against the official source. Sources SBA Learning Platform: https://learning.sba.gov/ SBA Business Guide: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/ business-guide SCORE: https://www.score.org/ SCORE resources: https://www.score.org/resources/ Indiana SBDC: https://isbdc.org/ IRS, Small Business and Self-Employed: https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed FTC, Business Guidance: https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance DigitalLearn: https://www.digitallearn.org/ Google Skillshop: https://skillshop.withgoogle.com/ Microsoft Learn: https://learn.microsoft.com/training/ HubSpot Academy: https://academy.hubspot.com/ Canva Design School: https://www.canva.com/designschool/ freeCodeCamp: https://www.freecodecamp.org/learn/ The Odin Project: https://www.theodinproject.com/ Kaggle Learn: https://www.kaggle.com/learn Harvard CS50x: https://cs50.harvard.edu/x/ MIT OpenCourseWare: https://ocw.mit.edu/ Elements of AI: https://www.elementsofai.com/ Hugging Face Learn: https://huggingface.co/learn Free training and template offers checked October 22, 2026. Access, certificates, and licences change; verify with the provider. Not legal or tax advice. Next: Free help in Indiana & the US →"},{"slug":"12-free-help-indiana.html","number":"12","short":"Free help","title":"Free help in Indiana & the US: real people, no sales pitch","group":"Learn & get help","headings":[{"id":"who-to-contact-first","text":"Who to contact first"},{"id":"the-free-help-map","text":"The free-help map"},{"id":"what-free-help-can-and-cannot-do","text":"What free help can and cannot do"},{"id":"how-to-get-real-value-from-a-30-minute-appointment","text":"How to get real value from a 30-minute appointment"},{"id":"a-short-outreach-email","text":"A short outreach email"},{"id":"scam-protection-in-plain-english","text":"Scam protection, in plain English"},{"id":"specialized-free-help","text":"Specialized free help"},{"id":"ai-helper-prepare-for-a-free-advising-session","text":"AI helper: prepare for a free advising session"},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources"}],"text":"Free help in Indiana & the US: real people, no sales pitch The job: Know where to get qualified, free or low-cost guidance before you guess, overpay, or fall for a scam. The best free resource is not a PDF—it's a person who has seen your situation before and will tell you what to do next. This chapter is education, not legal, tax, or financial advice. Free advisors can explain programs and offer general guidance; they are not your attorney, CPA, or financial advisor, and they cannot guarantee a loan, grant, contract, or approval. Verify eligibility and deadlines with the official agency. Never pay an unsolicited caller who says they can \"unlock\" a government grant. Who to contact first For most Indiana one-person businesses, the shortest path is: Indiana SBDC for no-cost business advising and a local point of contact: https://isbdc.org/ . SCORE for a free mentor and free workshops, especially if you want a long-term sounding board: https://www.score.org/ . SBA local assistance to find the resource partner that fits your situation: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/ . The official agency for filings, taxes, and licenses—INBiz, the Indiana Department of Revenue, or the IRS. Free advice does not replace an official filing. Free does not mean low quality. SCORE volunteers are often retired executives. SBDC advisors work with small businesses every day. Come prepared and you will get more than a generic brochure. The free-help map Resource Best for Cost Official starting point --- --- --- --- Indiana SBDC Indiana-specific advising, planning, referrals No cost for advising; some training may have a fee https://isbdc.org/ SCORE Mentoring, workshops, templates Free https://www.score.org/ SBA district offices Federal program questions, local referrals Free https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/ APEX Accelerators Government contracting questions Free or low cost https://www.apexaccelerators.us/ SBA Women's Business Centers Advising for women entrepreneurs Free or low cost https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/resource-partners/ womens-centers SBA Veterans Business Outreach Centers Advising for veterans and military spouses Free https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/resource-partners/ veterans-business-outreach-centers MBDA Business Centers Minority business development Free or low cost https://www.mbda.gov/ Indiana INBiz Official formation and business filings Government filing fees may apply https://inbiz.in.gov/ Indiana Department of Revenue State tax questions and registrations Free official guidance https://www.in.gov/dor/i-am-a/business-corp/ IRS Taxpayer Advocate Service Unresolved federal tax problems Free https://www.taxpayeradvocate.irs.gov/ Indiana State Bar Association Finding a licensed attorney Referral services; legal fees apply https://www.inbar.org/ Local public library Research databases, tech help, business books Free with a library card Ask your branch A note on \"free\" advising: SCORE and SBDC advising are designed to be no-cost, but always ask before a meeting whether any service has a fee. Legitimate organizations do not demand a payment to \"hold\" your free appointment or \"activate\" a grant. What free help can and cannot do A good advisor can: Explain how a program, form, or process works in plain English. Review your plan or numbers and point out gaps. Help you prepare questions for an attorney, CPA, or lender. Connect you with local programs, workshops, and peers. Tell you when a paid professional is genuinely worth it. An advisor cannot: Give you legal advice or sign off on your contract. Prepare or sign your tax return. Approve a loan, grant, or government contract. Guarantee that a business idea will work. Make a decision that is yours to make. If someone offers a guarantee, treats a free service as a sales funnel, or asks for sensitive information before you have verified who they are, stop and verify through the official website. How to get real value from a 30-minute appointment Before the meeting, write a one-page brief: What the business does, in one sentence. Who it serves and how it makes money. The specific decision or bottleneck you need help with. What you have already tried. The three questions you most need answered. Your timeline and rough budget. Bring or have ready: your business name and entity type, your website or draft, a simple revenue/expense picture, and any relevant document with sensitive details redacted. Do not send your Social Security number, EIN, bank credentials, or customer health information by ordinary email. During the meeting: Ask, \"What would you do next if this were your business?\" Ask, \"What am I not asking that I should be?\" Write down the advice and the source. Ask whether they can refer you to a specialist. After the meeting: send a two-line thank-you that summarizes the next step. Put the follow-up on your calendar. A short outreach email Subject: Request for a free advising session — [business name] Hi [organization], I'm the owner of [business name], a [one-sentence description] in [city], Indiana. I'm working on [specific decision or problem] and would like a 30-minute advising session. Current situation: [2–3 sentences, no sensitive data]. What I've tried: [1–2 sentences]. My three questions: [question] [question] [question] I'm available [two time windows]. I understand this is general business guidance, not legal or tax advice. Thank you. [Name] [Business website or phone] Keep it under 200 words. A clear, specific request gets a faster reply than \"I want to start a business, please advise.\" Scam protection, in plain English Government resources are free to access. Anyone charging you to \"apply for an EIN,\" \"file your LLC,\" or \"claim a free grant\" is selling a service or running a scam. Red flags: An unsolicited call, text, or DM saying you qualify for a \"free government grant.\" A fee before you can see the grant, loan, or program details. A website that looks like the SBA or INBiz but has a different URL. Pressure to pay today, or a \"deadline\" that disappears tomorrow. A request for your SSN, EIN, bank login, or a copy of your ID before you have verified the organization. A \"mentor\" who guarantees approvals, asks you to buy gift cards, or moves the conversation to a private messaging app. Paid \"certification\" or \"registration\" that is not required by any agency. Safer habits: Type the official address yourself: sba.gov , irs.gov , inbiz.in.gov , in.gov . Search the organization's name plus \"scam\" before paying anyone. Use a separate business email and a password manager. Ask an advisor you met through the SBDC or SCORE to review an offer before you sign. Report suspected fraud at https://reportfraud.ftc.gov/ and, for internet crime, https://www.ic3.gov/ . Specialized free help Veterans and military spouses: SBA Veterans Business Outreach Centers. Women entrepreneurs: SBA Women's Business Centers. Minority-owned businesses: MBDA Business Centers. Government contracting: APEX Accelerators. Exporting: SBA and the U.S. Commercial Service offer counseling and market information; start with the SBA local assistance page. Agriculture, childcare, and other regulated sectors: ask the SBDC for the agency that governs your industry before relying on general advice. Eligibility rules vary. A center that is not the right fit will usually refer you; that is still a useful call. AI helper: prepare for a free advising session I have a free 30-minute advising appointment with [SCORE / Indiana SBDC / SBA resource partner]. My business is [one sentence]. The decision I need help with is [describe, without sensitive data]. Produce: 1) a one-page brief I can read aloud, 2) eight specific questions ranked by importance, the documents I should bring, 4) three things I should not share in an email, and 5) a follow-up note template. Do not give legal, tax, or financial advice. Do not invent program names or eligibility rules. Use the output to prepare. Confirm every program detail with the official agency. Sources SBA local assistance: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/ SBA Business Guide: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/ business-guide SCORE: https://www.score.org/ Indiana SBDC: https://isbdc.org/ Indiana INBiz: https://inbiz.in.gov/ Indiana Secretary of State, business resources: https://www.in.gov/sos/business/ Indiana Department of Revenue, business: https://www.in.gov/dor/i-am-a/business-corp/ IRS, Small Business and Self-Employed: https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed IRS Taxpayer Advocate Service: https://www.taxpayeradvocate.irs.gov/ APEX Accelerators: https://www.apexaccelerators.us/ MBDA: https://www.mbda.gov/ Indiana State Bar Association: https://www.inbar.org/ FTC, Report Fraud: https://reportfraud.ftc.gov/ FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center: https://www.ic3.gov/ Program details checked October 22, 2026. Eligibility, hours, and services change; confirm with the organization. Not legal or tax advice. Next: AI helpers →"},{"slug":"13-ai-helpers.html","number":"13","short":"AI helpers","title":"AI helpers: drafts, not decisions","group":"AI, carefully","headings":[{"id":"start-with-the-right-kind-of-ai","text":"Start with the right kind of AI"},{"id":"set-up-privacy-before-you-get-productive","text":"Set up privacy before you get productive"},{"id":"a-redaction-checklist","text":"A redaction checklist"},{"id":"local-ai-free-private-and-a-little-nerdy","text":"Local AI: free, private, and a little nerdy"},{"id":"free-ai-coding-helpers","text":"Free AI coding helpers"},{"id":"prompt-patterns-that-work-for-a-tiny-business","text":"Prompt patterns that work for a tiny business"},{"id":"a-reusable-business-helper-prompt","text":"A reusable business helper prompt"},{"id":"the-verification-checklist","text":"The verification checklist"},{"id":"what-ai-should-not-decide","text":"What AI should not decide"},{"id":"ai-helper-audit-your-own-ai-use","text":"AI helper: audit your own AI use"},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources"}],"text":"AI helpers: drafts, not decisions The job: Use free AI to get unstuck on drafting, summarizing, planning, and explaining—without handing it your secrets, your judgment, or your legal and tax decisions. AI is a fast intern with no memory of your business and a habit of sounding certain when it is wrong. This is education, not legal, tax, accounting, or medical advice. AI output can be inaccurate, outdated, biased, or invented. Verify facts, numbers, links, prices, and policy details against official sources. Never use AI to diagnose or treat a patient, give medical advice, or replace a licensed professional. Never put protected health information, client data, account numbers, EINs, passwords, or API keys into a chat you do not control. Start with the right kind of AI For a one-person business, \"AI\" usually means one of three things: Kind What it is Good first use Main caution --- --- --- --- Hosted chatbot A website or app that runs a model for you Drafting, summarizing, explaining, brainstorming Your input leaves your device; check data controls Local AI A model running on your own computer Private drafting and organizing Hardware limits; still verify output Coding assistant AI inside an editor or terminal Learning code, fixing small errors Review every change; never expose secrets Pick one hosted assistant and learn it well. ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Microsoft Copilot, and Perplexity all offer free tiers, but limits and terms change. The best free assistant is the one whose privacy settings you understand and whose answers you actually check. Set up privacy before you get productive Do this once, before you paste anything real. Use a business-only account. Do not mix a personal account full of family photos and banking emails with your business drafts. Find the data controls. Look for settings about model training, chat history, and \"improve the service.\" Turn off training or data improvement where the provider offers it. Terms differ; read the current policy. Use temporary or incognito chats for one-off questions when available. Assume anything you paste could be stored. The safest sensitive document is one you never upload. Redact before you paste. Replace names with [CLIENT] , amounts with [AMOUNT] , addresses with [CITY] , and account numbers with [REDACTED] . Turn on two-factor authentication for the account. Keep a simple rule: no SSNs, EINs, bank logins, passwords, API keys, medical records, contracts you don't own, or other people's private information. Free consumer plans are not a secure records system. If you need a signed business agreement, use document storage, not a chat history. A redaction checklist Before pasting, scan for: Names, email addresses, phone numbers, and home addresses Social Security numbers, EINs, tax IDs, and dates of birth Bank and card numbers, routing numbers, and account balances Passwords, recovery codes, API keys, and access tokens Patient information, diagnoses, prescriptions, and insurance IDs Unpublished book text, client materials, or someone else's contract Anything you would not want a stranger to read If removing the detail makes the prompt useless, the task probably needs a local tool or a human professional—not a public chatbot. Local AI: free, private, and a little nerdy Local tools run a model on your own computer. Nothing is sent to a cloud service, which is appealing for sensitive drafts. The trade-off is hardware, setup, electricity, and model quality. Worth knowing: Ollama — a beginner-friendly way to run models locally. LM Studio — a desktop app with a chat interface and model downloads. llama.cpp — the lower-level engine many local tools build on. Open WebUI — a self-hosted chat interface for local or hosted models. A practical minimum is a reasonably recent computer with 16 GB of RAM for small models; larger models need more. A small local model can organize notes and draft routine text. It is not automatically safer to trust—it can still hallucinate. It is safer with data , not necessarily with facts . Do not self-host on the public internet just to \"have AI.\" If you expose a chat interface without authentication, you may expose your files. Local means local. Free AI coding helpers If you want to learn to build a small tool or fix your own website, these are legitimate starting points. Free plans and limits change constantly. GitHub Copilot Free — AI suggestions inside common editors for eligible GitHub accounts: https://github.com/features/copilot . Gemini CLI — an open-source command-line assistant with a free tier for Google accounts: https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli . Cline — an open-source coding agent for VS Code; you provide the model: https://cline.bot/ . Continue — open-source AI assistance you can configure with local or hosted models: https://continue.dev/ . Aider — open-source pair programming in the terminal; model usage may cost money: https://aider.chat/ . OpenRouter — one API for many models, with some free models and usage limits: https://openrouter.ai/ . Coding safety rules: Never paste API keys into a chat or commit them to a repository. Review every code change before running it. AI can introduce security bugs. Don't give an agent broad access to your email, bank, or file system until you understand its permissions. Test on a copy before changing a live site. Keep backups and version control. Agents and MCP tools can be genuinely useful, but they can also take actions. A beginner should start with read-only suggestions, not an autonomous agent with your passwords. Prompt patterns that work for a tiny business Job Prompt shape Verify --- --- --- Setup plan \"Ask me five questions, then give a 7-step plan under 30 minutes per step\" Tool limits and prices Invoice reminder \"Warm, under 110 words, one clear next step, no invented late fee\" Due date and contract terms CRM notes \"Summarize only the facts I provide; mark missing facts TBD\" Names and commitments Welcome email \"Four short emails, no fake scarcity, one CTA each\" Claims and footer address Homepage copy \"Plain English, one primary CTA, no invented testimonials\" Every factual claim Design brief \"Propose colors, system fonts, and five assets; tell me to test contrast\" Accessibility contrast Form cleanup \"Remove unnecessary questions; flag sensitive data\" Data you actually need Contract questions \"Explain this excerpt and list questions for an attorney\" A licensed attorney Learning plan \"45-minute practice-first session with a self-check\" Official course details Monthly close \"Sort anonymized transactions; list ambiguous items as questions\" Your accountant A reusable business helper prompt Save this as a starting point. Change the role and constraints, not the privacy rules. You are a calm, practical assistant for a one-person business. Use plain English and short paragraphs. Ask before assuming. Label every assumption as [assumption]. Do not invent facts, prices, laws, links, testimonials, or statistics. If you are unsure, say so and tell me what to verify. Never ask me for passwords, account numbers, EINs, health information, or other sensitive data. Do not give legal, tax, medical, or financial advice; instead, list questions for a licensed professional. Keep outputs short enough to use today. Then add the task. The constraints do a lot of work. The verification checklist Before you use any AI output: Facts: Can you find the claim on an official or primary source? Numbers: Did you recalculate totals and dates yourself? Links: Does the link load, and is it the official page? Law and tax: Did a licensed professional confirm the rule for your situation? Health: Is this education only, with no patient-specific advice? Privacy: Did you remove names, IDs, and sensitive details? Tone: Does it sound like you, or like a generic brochure? Responsibility: Are you willing to stand behind the final version? If the answer to the last question is no, don't send it. What AI should not decide Whether an expense is deductible, or how to classify your LLC for tax. Whether a contract is enforceable or safe to sign. Whether a patient should receive a treatment or a diagnosis. Whether a marketing claim is legal or substantiated. Whether a person is trustworthy, hirable, or creditworthy. What your prices, boundaries, or values should be. AI can help you prepare better questions for the humans who carry that responsibility. AI helper: audit your own AI use Audit my AI workflow as a cautious operations assistant. I will describe the tools I use and the kinds of information I paste, without sharing actual sensitive data. Identify: 1) places where I might be exposing personal or business data, 2) tasks where a local model or a human professional is a better fit, 3) three prompts I should rewrite to include verification steps, and 4) a one-page monthly AI hygiene checklist. Do not claim that any setup is legally compliant. Do not ask for secrets. Run this audit once a quarter, or whenever you add a new tool. Sources Ollama: https://ollama.com/ LM Studio: https://lmstudio.ai/ llama.cpp: https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp Open WebUI: https://github.com/open-webui/open-webui GitHub Copilot: https://github.com/features/copilot Gemini CLI: https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli Cline: https://cline.bot/ Continue: https://continue.dev/ Aider: https://aider.chat/ OpenRouter: https://openrouter.ai/ FTC, protecting personal information: https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/protecting-personal-information-guide-business HHS, HIPAA privacy resources (background for healthcare-adjacent work): https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/index.html Free plans, model names, and data terms checked October 22, 2026. They change often; verify with the provider. Not legal, tax, or medical advice. Next: 90-day setup plan →"},{"slug":"14-90-day-plan.html","number":"14","short":"90-day plan","title":"The 90-day plan: one small session a week","group":"Put it together","headings":[{"id":"the-rules","text":"The rules"},{"id":"the-13-week-map","text":"The 13-week map"},{"id":"what-each-session-actually-produces","text":"What each session actually produces"},{"id":"the-maintenance-rhythm-after-90-days","text":"The maintenance rhythm after 90 days"},{"id":"the-low-energy-version","text":"The low-energy version"},{"id":"if-you-only-do-three-things","text":"If you only do three things"},{"id":"a-printable-session-checklist","text":"A printable session checklist"},{"id":"ai-helper-adapt-the-plan-to-your-real-life","text":"AI helper: adapt the plan to your real life"},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources"}],"text":"The 90-day plan: one small session a week The job: Turn this guide into a working business system without losing a weekend. One 45–60 minute session per week, one real outcome per session. If a week goes sideways, you have not failed; you have a plan with a next step. This is education, not legal, tax, or financial advice. The plan organizes free tools and learning. It does not decide your entity, taxes, contracts, insurance, or licenses. Book a free SBDC or SCORE session early, and use a licensed professional for decisions that matter. The rules One bottleneck at a time. Do not sign up for every tool in week one. Use real work. A real invoice, a real contact, a real page. Practice data teaches nothing. Stop at \"working,\" not \"perfect.\" You can polish after the business has a pulse. Export before you depend. Test the exit door while the tool is still free. If you miss a week, skip don't stack. Do the next session; don't do two in one sitting and resent the plan. The 13-week map Week Session Done when Start here --- --- --- --- 1 Clean drawer Business email, password manager, 2FA, three folders, one-sentence description Start here 2 Money home Wave (or a spreadsheet) set up, bank connected or manual entries started, 10 transactions categorized Accounting 3 First invoice One template, clear terms, one test invoice, payment method understood Invoicing 4 People system Spreadsheet or HubSpot Free, five real contacts, a next action on each, 15-minute weekly review booked CRM 5 Website v1 Home, About, Offer, Contact, and Privacy pages live on a free host; every link tested Website & hosting 6 Brand kit One-page brand sheet, wordmark, social image, link preview, invoice header, contrast checked Design 7 Booking + forms One booking type connected to a calendar, one short form tested end to end Scheduling, Forms 8 Email list Signup form, consent language, welcome email, unsubscribe tested, list exported Email marketing 9 Documents One low-risk document template, test signature, completed copy saved and backed up E-signature 10 Ask for help SBDC or SCORE appointment booked, one-page brief written, top questions listed Free help 11 Learn + AI setup One 45-minute training session applied to a real task, AI privacy settings reviewed, redaction rules written Free training, AI helpers 12 Backup drill Books, contacts, email list, invoices, website, and documents exported; one restore test completed Start here 13 Review + rhythm Current free-plan limits checked, one upgrade chosen or deliberately declined, recurring maintenance sessions booked Sources & fine print A realistic time budget: 45–60 minutes for the session, plus a few minutes during the week to gather information. Week 5 may take two sessions if you are learning hosting from scratch. That is normal. What each session actually produces Weeks 1–4: the foundation. By the end of week 4 you should be able to answer: Where does business email live? Where does money go? How do I ask for payment? Who am I following up with, and when? If those answers are clear, the rest is easier. Weeks 5–8: the front door. You do not need a perfect brand. You need a page that explains what you do, a way to contact you, a way to book you, and a way to stay in touch with permission. Publish before you feel ready, then fix what confuses real people. Weeks 9–12: the back office. This is where free tools earn their keep: simple signatures, free advising, one useful skill, and a backup you have actually restored. A backup you have never tested is a hope, not a backup. Week 13: the review. Open every tool's pricing page. Compare your usage to the limit. Cancel what you don't use. Decide whether one paid tool would save more time than it costs—and write down why. \"No\" is a valid answer. The maintenance rhythm after 90 days Rhythm Time What happens --- --- --- Weekly 15 min Relationship review: next actions, waiting-on, one useful follow-up Monthly 25 min Money date: reconcile, categorize, check invoices, export CSV and P&L Quarterly 30 min Export all data, check free-plan limits, review AI privacy, update links and prices Annually 60 min Review tools, insurance, licenses, tax questions, and whether the business still fits you Put these on the calendar with the same seriousness as a client meeting. The business is also a client. The low-energy version Some weeks you will have 15 minutes and a flat battery. Do this instead of skipping entirely: Tiny week: open the tool, do one transaction, write one next action, or send one follow-up. Admin-only week: download one export and put it in the backup folder. No-buy week: cancel one unused subscription or decline one upsell. Ask-for-help week: send one short email to the SBDC, SCORE, or a mentor. Rest week: read one chapter and write one question. That counts. ADHD-friendly, low-energy productivity is not a moral failure. It is a design requirement. Smaller sessions are allowed. If you only do three things Separate the money and keep a monthly money date. Put a one-page website and a way to contact you online. Book free human help before you pay for advice. Everything else can wait until a real limit appears. A printable session checklist THIS WEEK'S SESSION [ ] Pick one bottleneck [ ] Set a 45-minute timer [ ] Open only the tools for this session [ ] Do the real task, not the tutorial [ ] Export or save the result [ ] Write one next action with a date [ ] Close the laptop DONE LOOKS LIKE What I can now do without looking it up: What still blocks me: Who I will ask: When my next session is: AI helper: adapt the plan to your real life I want to follow a 13-week free-tool setup plan for a one-person [type] business. My constraints are: [hours per week], [caregiving or health considerations], [budget], and [current tools]. Rewrite the plan as 13 sessions of 45 minutes or less. Keep one outcome per session. Mark any session that can be split. Add a 15-minute low-energy fallback for each week. Do not recommend paid tools unless I ask. Do not give legal, tax, or financial advice. End with a one-page maintenance calendar for the first month after week 13. Verify tool availability and prices yourself. The plan should fit your life, not the other way around. Sources SBA Business Guide: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/ business-guide SBA local counseling: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/ SCORE: https://www.score.org/ Indiana SBDC: https://isbdc.org/ IRS, Small Business and Self-Employed: https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed FTC, Business Guidance: https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance Plan checked October 22, 2026. Tool details live in each chapter; verify current limits before relying on them. Not legal or tax advice. Next: Sources & fine print →"},{"slug":"15-sources-and-fine-print.html","number":"15","short":"Sources & fine print","title":"Sources & fine print: how to check a freebie yourself","group":"Put it together","headings":[{"id":"how-this-guide-was-checked","text":"How this guide was checked"},{"id":"the-master-source-list","text":"The master source list"},{"id":"the-freebie-scorecard","text":"The freebie scorecard"},{"id":"scam-and-trap-patterns","text":"Scam and trap patterns"},{"id":"how-to-re-check-a-freebie-in-10-minutes","text":"How to re-check a freebie in 10 minutes"},{"id":"what-this-guide-does-not-cover","text":"What this guide does not cover"},{"id":"licence-credit-and-disclaimer","text":"Licence, credit, and disclaimer"},{"id":"change-log","text":"Change log"},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources"}],"text":"Sources & fine print: how to check a freebie yourself The job: Verify the details that matter before you build a workflow on them, recognize a real free resource from a sales trap, and know the limits of this guide. Free plans mutate. Official pages are the source of truth. This guide is education, not legal, tax, accounting, financial, or medical advice. Nothing here creates an attorney-client, accountant-client, or fiduciary relationship. Business structure, taxes, contracts, insurance, licenses, and regulated data depend on facts we don't have. Consult a licensed professional in your jurisdiction before making decisions. How this guide was checked Free-plan details and links were checked against official pricing and government pages on October 22–23, 2026 . No affiliate links are used. No tool or agency paid for placement. Asdale Dynamics, LLC is not affiliated with any tool, agency, or program listed. Where a detail is likely to change—prices, limits, AI allowances, eligibility—the chapter says so and points to the official page. This is a starting map, not a warranty. Re-check anything you plan to depend on. The master source list Chapter Free starting point Official page to re-check --- --- --- Accounting Wave Starter https://www.waveapps.com/pricing Invoicing Zoho Invoice Free, or Wave https://www.zoho.com/invoice/pricing/ CRM HubSpot Free, or a spreadsheet https://www.hubspot.com/pricing/crm Email marketing MailerLite Free, Brevo Free https://www.mailerlite.com/pricing , https://www.brevo.com/pricing/ Website & hosting GitHub Pages, Netlify Free https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/github-pages-limits , https://www.netlify.com/pricing/ Design Canva Free https://www.canva.com/pricing/ Scheduling Cal.com Free, Calendly Free https://cal.com/pricing , https://calendly.com/pricing Forms Tally Free, Google Forms https://tally.so/pricing E-signature DocuSeal Free https://www.docuseal.com/pricing Legal templates INBiz, IRS, SBA, SCORE https://inbiz.in.gov/ , https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs , https://www.sba.gov/counseling/ business-guide Training & templates SBA Learning Platform, SCORE, freeCodeCamp https://learning.sba.gov/ , https://www.score.org/ , https://www.freecodecamp.org/learn/ Free help Indiana SBDC, SCORE, SBA https://isbdc.org/ , https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/ AI helpers Free hosted assistants, local AI, free coding tools Each provider's current pricing and privacy page 90-day plan This guide's weekly rhythm The chapters above Start here One email, one folder, one calendar Your own setup notes The freebie scorecard Before you build a habit around a free tool, score it on these ten points. Any \"no\" is a conversation with yourself, not automatically a dealbreaker. Real free tier: Can you do the core job forever, or is this a short trial? Core-job completeness: Does the free plan require a paid add-on for the thing you actually need? Export: Can you get your data out in a usable format? Have you tested it? Limits: Do you know the contact, document, storage, send, or usage caps? Commercial use: Does the licence allow business use, client work, or resale? Check the actual licence. Privacy and data use: What does the provider collect, and does it train on your data? Is the tool appropriate for the information you're putting in it? Security: Two-factor authentication, access controls, and a credible security page. Support: Can you get help without paying, and is there a real documentation page? Longevity: Is the provider established, and do you have a replacement plan if the free tier changes? Total cost: Domain, payment processing, storage, exports, upsells, and your time. \"Free\" is not the same as \"no cost.\" The exit test: Before you store anything important, find the export button, export a sample, and open the file. If you cannot leave, do not move in. Scam and trap patterns Free tools and government programs attract copycats. These patterns are common: \"Free government grant\" calls or texts. SBA does not call you out of the blue to award a grant. Grants are specific, competitive, and documented on official sites. Never pay a fee to \"release\" a grant. Paid EIN or LLC filing services. You can apply for an EIN free at the IRS and file formation documents at INBiz. Third-party services can be legitimate, but cold calls and look-alike sites are not the place to start. Look-alike government domains. A site with an SBA logo and a slightly wrong URL is not the SBA. Type sba.gov , irs.gov , inbiz.in.gov , or in.gov yourself. Free trials with automatic renewal. Put a cancellation reminder on your calendar before the trial ends. Use a virtual card or a payment method with a low limit if available. \"Lifetime\" deals with no export. A lifetime price on a tool that holds your only copy of customer data is a hostage situation, not a bargain. Fake mentors and guaranteed approvals. A legitimate free advisor does not demand payment, gift cards, crypto, or your bank login. Verify the organization through the SBDC, SCORE, or SBA. \"Free\" templates you cannot use commercially. Read the licence. Personal-use-only templates are not a business foundation. AI tools that ask for credentials. Never paste passwords, API keys, or recovery codes into a chat. A legitimate AI product does not need them to draft an email. Unsolicited tax or bookkeeping help. Verify the preparer's credentials and the official filing channel. A confident stranger is not a CPA. Report fraud: https://reportfraud.ftc.gov/ and https://www.ic3.gov/ . Report suspected impersonation to the agency being imitated. How to re-check a freebie in 10 minutes Once a quarter, for each tool you depend on: Open the official pricing page and note today's date. Check the limits against your current usage. Read the data-use and training settings. Test an export and open the file. Check whether the plan still covers the core job. Look for a replacement that can import your data. Update your own notes—not just this guide. If you adapt this guide for your audience, keep the checked date visible and re-verify the links before you publish. A confidently outdated freebie list is worse than no list. What this guide does not cover Payroll, benefits, and multi-state employment. Inventory, manufacturing, and complex e-commerce operations. Insurance selection, loan underwriting, and grant applications. International tax, privacy, and business formation. Regulated industries, including healthcare compliance beyond the education-only guardrails here. Securities, investments, and financial advice. Any guarantee about free-plan longevity, tool quality, or eligibility. For those, use the official agency and a licensed professional. Free tools are infrastructure; they are not professional judgment. Licence, credit, and disclaimer This guide and site are published by ASDALE DYNAMICS, LLC , an Indiana company focused on beginner-friendly AI education, books, and practical tools. Licence: The text and site code in this repository are released under the MIT License. See LICENSE. Tool names, logos, and trademarks belong to their owners. Credit: If you reuse substantial portions, credit \"Asdale Dynamics, LLC — Free Tools to Run a Small Business / LLC.\" No affiliate links: No link in this guide is a paid placement. No warranty: The guide is provided \"as is,\" without warranty of any kind. Free plans and government pages can change without notice. No professional relationship: Reading this guide does not make Asdale Dynamics, LLC your lawyer, accountant, or advisor. Change log 2026-10-22: Chapters 00–10 drafted; free-plan details and official links checked. 2026-10-23: Chapters 11–15 added (training, free help, AI, 90-day plan, sources); static site built; automated link and build checks added. 2026-10-25: External links audited. Repaired moved SBA business guide, Learning Platform, Women's Business Center, and Veterans Business Outreach Center links; switched the Indiana e-signature citation to the official IGA code; added Google Forms' current storage and response limits. When the official pages change, update the chapter and this log. Keep the guide honest rather than permanent. Sources SBA: https://www.sba.gov/ SBA local assistance: https://www.sba.gov/counseling/local-assistance/ SCORE: https://www.score.org/ Indiana SBDC: https://isbdc.org/ Indiana INBiz: https://inbiz.in.gov/ Indiana Secretary of State, business: https://www.in.gov/sos/business/ Indiana Department of Revenue, business: https://www.in.gov/dor/i-am-a/business-corp/ IRS, Small Business and Self-Employed: https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed IRS, EIN: https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/apply-for-an-employer-identification-number-ein-online FTC, Business Guidance: https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance FTC, Report Fraud: https://reportfraud.ftc.gov/ FBI IC3: https://www.ic3.gov/ HHS, HIPAA privacy: https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/index.html Checked October 22–25, 2026. Re-verify before relying on any free plan or government program. Not legal, tax, or medical advice. Back to Start here ↑"}]};
